./data/screening_codex_full_2026-07-06/role_logs/adjudicator/batch_0060.stderr.log:71:[{"record_id":"rec_001262","title":"Modeling and predicting single-cell multi-gene perturbation responses with scLAMBDA","abstract":"Understanding cellular responses to genetic perturbations is essential for understanding gene regulation and phenotype formation. While high-throughput single-cell RNA-sequencing has facilitated detailed profiling of heterogeneous transcriptional responses to perturbations at the single-cell level, there remains a pressing need for computational models that can decode the mechanisms driving these responses and accurately predict outcomes to prioritize target genes for experimental design. Here, we present scLAMBDA, a deep generative learning framework designed to model and predict single-cell transcriptional responses to genetic perturbations, including single-gene and combinatorial multi-gene perturbations. By leveraging gene embeddings derived from large language models, scLAMBDA effectively integrates prior biological knowledge and disentangles basal cell states from perturbation-specific salient representations. Through comprehensive evaluations on multiple single-cell CRISPR Perturb-seq datasets, scLAMBDA consistently outperformed state-of-the-art methods in predicting perturbation outcomes, achieving higher prediction accuracy. Notably, scLAMBDA demonstrated robust generalization to unseen target genes and perturbations, and its predictions captured both average expression changes and the heterogeneity of single-cell responses. Furthermore, its predictions enable diverse downstream analyses, including the identification of differentially expressed genes and the exploration of genetic interactions, demonstrating its utility and versatility.","doi":"10.1101/2024.12.04.626878","year":"2024","venue":"bioRxiv","sources":["google_scholar","semantic_scholar"],"first_pass_outputs":{"scope_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_001262","paper_type":"application_wrapper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"application_wrapper","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"The model uses “gene embeddings derived from large language models,” which are external LLM-derived features rather than a substantive text model within the candidate system. That makes it an application wrapper around text-derived embeddings for a bio task.","evidence_snippet":"“leveraging gene embeddings derived from large language models”"},"architecture_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_001262","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","generative_model_present":"yes","foundation_model_evidence":"unclear","primary_exclusion_code":"none","uncertainty_reason":"foundation_model_evidence_unclear","decision_rationale":"The abstract explicitly calls scLAMBDA a deep generative learning framework for modeling perturbation responses. It suggests transferability to unseen genes and perturbations, but does not clearly establish broad foundation-model pretraining.","evidence_snippet":"\"deep generative learning framework\" and \"robust generalization to unseen target genes and perturbations\""},"python_gate":{"provisional_decision":"ADJUDICATE","provisional_code":"application_wrapper","adjudication_reason":"criterion_unclear_or_reviewer_conflict","paper_type":"application_wrapper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","generative_model_present":"yes","foundation_model_evidence":"unclear"}}},{"record_id":"rec_001267","title":"Multimodal Omics Approaches to Aging and Age-Related Diseases","abstract":"Ji Q(#)(1)(2)(3)(4)(5), Jiang X(#)(1)(2)(3)(4)(5), Wang M(6)(3), Xin Z(1)(2)(3)(4)(5), Zhang W(6)(3)(4)(7), Qu J(8)(2)(3)(4)(5), Liu GH(1)(2)(3)(4)(5)(7)(9)(10).","doi":"10.1007/s43657-023-00125-x","year":"2024","venue":"Phenomics","sources":["semantic_scholar"],"first_pass_outputs":{"scope_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_001267","paper_type":"review_editorial","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"review_editorial","uncertainty_reason":"paper_type_unclear","decision_rationale":"The record is titled like a broad synthesis on multimodal omics and provides no primary-study abstract content beyond authors. It does not describe a text-bio model.","evidence_snippet":"\"Multimodal Omics Approaches to Aging and Age-Related Diseases\""},"architecture_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_001267","paper_type":"review_editorial","generative_model_present":"unclear","foundation_model_evidence":"unclear","primary_exclusion_code":"review_editorial","uncertainty_reason":"paper_type_unclear","decision_rationale":"The record provides only a title and author list, with no abstract content about a model. The title suggests a review-style topic, but the evidence is insufficient to assess generative or foundation-model criteria.","evidence_snippet":"title only: \"Multimodal Omics Approaches to Aging and Age-Related Diseases\"; abstract field contains author names only"},"python_gate":{"provisional_decision":"ADJUDICATE","provisional_code":"review_editorial","adjudication_reason":"criterion_unclear_or_reviewer_conflict","paper_type":"review_editorial","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","generative_model_present":"unclear","foundation_model_evidence":"unclear"}}},{"record_id":"rec_001270","title":"DNAMotifTokenizer: Towards Biologically Informed Tokenization of Genomic Sequences","abstract":"DNA language models have advanced genomics, but their downstream performance varies widely due to differences in tokenization, pretraining data, and architecture. We argue that a major bottleneck lies in tokenizing sparse and unevenly distributed DNA sequence motifs, which are critical for accurate and interpretable models. To investigate, we systematically benchmark k-mer and Byte-Pair Encoding (BPE) tokenizers under controlled pretraining budget, evaluating across multiple downstream tasks from five datasets. We find that tokenizer choice induces task-specific trade-offs, and that vocabulary size and tokenizer training data strongly influence the biological knowledge captured. Notably, BPE tokenizers achieve strong performance when trained on smaller but biologically significant data. Building on these insights, we introduce DNAMotifTokenizer, which directly incorporates domain knowledge of DNA sequence motifs into the tokenization process. DNAMotifTokenizer consistently outperforms BPE across diverse benchmarks, demonstrating that knowledge-infused tokenization is crucial for learning powerful, interpretable, and generalizable genomic representations.","doi":"","year":"2025","venue":"","sources":["arxiv","semantic_scholar"],"first_pass_outputs":{"scope_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_001270","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"yes","primary_exclusion_code":"none","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"The abstract centers on DNA language models, tokenization, and pretraining with biologically informed motifs. This is a substantive language-model-style genomic modeling paper with an explicit text-bio bridge.","evidence_snippet":"\"DNA language models\", \"tokenization\", \"knowledge-infused tokenization\""},"architecture_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_001270","paper_type":"benchmark_resource","generative_model_present":"no","foundation_model_evidence":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"benchmark_resource","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"This paper benchmarks tokenizers under a controlled pretraining budget and introduces a tokenization method, not a generative model itself. The abstract discusses downstream benchmark performance rather than a foundation-model candidate.","evidence_snippet":"\"systematically benchmark k-mer and Byte-Pair Encoding (BPE) tokenizers\" and \"evaluate across multiple downstream tasks\""},"python_gate":{"provisional_decision":"ADJUDICATE","provisional_code":"benchmark_resource","adjudication_reason":"criterion_unclear_or_reviewer_conflict","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"yes","generative_model_present":"no","foundation_model_evidence":"no"}}},{"record_id":"rec_001272","title":"AI for precision medicine: Integrating machine learning across genomics, therapeutics, and clinical governance","abstract":"Precision Medicine (PM) is a complete paradigm shift in healthcare in terms of customized care based on individual differences in genomics, environment, and lifestyle. This objective is limited by the complexity and sheer amount of multi-modal data created by such sources as genomics, Electronic Health Records (EHRs), and the Internet of Medical Things (IoMT). Artificial Intelligence (AI), Machine Learning (ML), and Deep Learning (DL) are becoming critical means of processing and understanding these heterogeneous data streams to give predictive and therapeutic insights. This empirical review is a synthesis of recent discoveries, and it shows that AI can be used in high-accuracy predictive diagnostics, including with a maximum Area Under the Curve (AUC) of 0.97 with complex cardiac analysis and optimized treatment using pharmacogenomics, which has been demonstrated to decrease adverse drug reactions by 35% in high-risk geriatric patients. Nonetheless, the mass use of this technology has been severely socio-technical: ensuring that the data will not be re-identified, reducing the risk of bias in the algorithms based on past health disparities, and creating clear accountability mechanisms of AI-aided clinical judgments. Effective translation requires synergistic emphasis on technological innovation (e.g., Explainable AI and Federated Transfer Learning) and effective clinical governance (e.g., compulsory data standardization and updated informed consent procedures) to make the concept of personalized healthcare a reality.","doi":"10.30574/wjaets.2025.17.2.1493","year":"2025","venue":"World Journal of Advanced Engineering Technology and Sciences","sources":["semantic_scholar"],"first_pass_outputs":{"scope_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_001272","paper_type":"review_editorial","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"review_editorial","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"The abstract explicitly says this is an empirical review of AI in precision medicine. It surveys genomics and clinical applications rather than presenting a candidate text-bio model.","evidence_snippet":"\"This empirical review is a synthesis of recent discoveries\""},"architecture_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_001272","paper_type":"review_editorial","generative_model_present":"unclear","foundation_model_evidence":"unclear","primary_exclusion_code":"review_editorial","uncertainty_reason":"paper_type_unclear","decision_rationale":"The abstract explicitly calls this an empirical review and synthesis of recent discoveries. It does not describe a candidate generative model or provide evidence of foundation-model characteristics.","evidence_snippet":"\"This empirical review is a synthesis of recent discoveries\""},"python_gate":{"provisional_decision":"ADJUDICATE","provisional_code":"review_editorial","adjudication_reason":"criterion_unclear_or_reviewer_conflict","paper_type":"review_editorial","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","generative_model_present":"unclear","foundation_model_evidence":"unclear"}}},{"record_id":"rec_001275","title":"Scaling medical AI across clinical contexts.","abstract":"To function safely and effectively, medical AI models must adapt automatically to differences in users, health systems, geographies, diseases and populations. This Perspective proposes context switching as the defining paradigm of medical AI, outlining early strategies and opportunities for development. Medical artificial intelligence (AI) tools, including clinical language models, vision–language models and multimodal health record models, are used to summarize clinical notes, answer questions and support decisions. Their adaptation to new populations, specialties or care settings often relies on fine-tuning, prompting or retrieval from external knowledge bases. These strategies can scale poorly and risk contextual errors—outputs that appear plausible but miss critical patient or situational information. We envision context switching as an emergent solution. Context switching adjusts model reasoning at inference, without retraining. Generative models can tailor outputs to patient biology, care setting or disease. Multimodal models can switch between notes, laboratory results, imaging and genomics, even when some data are missing or delayed. Agent models can coordinate tools and roles based on task and user context. In each case, context switching enables medical AI to adapt across specialties, populations and geographies. This approach requires advances in data design, model architectures and evaluation frameworks, and establishes a foundation for medical AI that scales to an infinite number of contexts, while remaining reliable and suited to real-world care.","doi":"10.1038/s41591-025-04184-7","year":"2026","venue":"Nature Medicine","sources":["semantic_scholar","springernature"],"first_pass_outputs":{"scope_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_001275","paper_type":"review_editorial","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"yes","primary_exclusion_code":"review_editorial","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"This is a Perspective on medical AI, not a primary model paper. It discusses clinical language models and models tailored to patient biology, but it is editorial commentary.","evidence_snippet":"\"This Perspective proposes context switching\"; \"clinical language models\"; \"tailor outputs to patient biology\""},"architecture_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_001275","paper_type":"review_editorial","generative_model_present":"unclear","foundation_model_evidence":"unclear","primary_exclusion_code":"review_editorial","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"This is a Perspective on scaling medical AI, not a primary model paper. It discusses generative models conceptually but does not describe a specific candidate model.","evidence_snippet":"“This Perspective proposes context switching”"},"python_gate":{"provisional_decision":"ADJUDICATE","provisional_code":"review_editorial","adjudication_reason":"criterion_unclear_or_reviewer_conflict","paper_type":"review_editorial","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"yes","generative_model_present":"unclear","foundation_model_evidence":"unclear"}}},{"record_id":"rec_001277","title":"WITHDRAWN: OKR-Cell: Open World Knowledge Aided Single-Cell Foundation Model with Robust Cross-Modal Cell-Language Pre-training","abstract":"Withdrawal statement\n                The authors have withdrawn this manuscript because of a duplicate posting of a preprint on arXiv. Therefore, the authors do not wish this work to be cited as reference for the project. If you have any questions, please contact the corresponding author.\n                The original preprint can be found at arXiv:2601.05648","doi":"10.64898/2026.01.09.698573","year":"2026","venue":"bioRxiv","sources":["semantic_scholar"],"first_pass_outputs":{"scope_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_001277","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"yes","primary_exclusion_code":"none","uncertainty_reason":"mixed_signals","decision_rationale":"The title presents a single-cell foundation model with cross-modal cell-language pre-training, but the abstract is only a withdrawal notice. That makes the record mixed, though the title supports a primary model with text-bio bridging.","evidence_snippet":"title: \"Open World Knowledge Aided Single-Cell Foundation Model with Robust Cross-Modal Cell-Language Pre-training\"; abstract: \"The authors have withdrawn this manuscript\""},"architecture_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_001277","paper_type":"unclear","generative_model_present":"unclear","foundation_model_evidence":"unclear","primary_exclusion_code":"EC4_no_foundation_model_evidence","uncertainty_reason":"paper_type_unclear","decision_rationale":"The record is only a withdrawal statement and does not describe a model. There is no abstract evidence for generative behavior or foundation-model characteristics.","evidence_snippet":"“The authors have withdrawn this manuscript”"},"python_gate":{"provisional_decision":"ADJUDICATE","provisional_code":"EC4_no_foundation_model_evidence","adjudication_reason":"criterion_unclear_or_reviewer_conflict","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"yes","generative_model_present":"unclear","foundation_model_evidence":"unclear"}}}]
./data/screening_codex_full_2026-07-06/role_logs/adjudicator/batch_0060.stderr.log:108:{"results":[{"record_id":"rec_001262","paper_type":"application_wrapper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","generative_model_present":"yes","foundation_model_evidence":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"application_wrapper","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"The abstract describes a deep generative model for single-cell perturbation prediction, but the only text signal is external gene embeddings from large language models. That supports an application wrapper rather than a substantive text-bio foundation model.","evidence_snippet":"“leveraging gene embeddings derived from large language models” and “deep generative learning framework”"},{"record_id":"rec_001267","paper_type":"review_editorial","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","generative_model_present":"unclear","foundation_model_evidence":"unclear","primary_exclusion_code":"review_editorial","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"The record is a topic title with an author list only and no primary-study abstract content. It is best treated as a review/editorial, not a model paper.","evidence_snippet":"“Multimodal Omics Approaches to Aging and Age-Related Diseases” plus author list only"},{"record_id":"rec_001270","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","generative_model_present":"no","foundation_model_evidence":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"EC2_no_text_component","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"The abstract is about genomic tokenization and benchmarking for DNA language models, but it does not describe a text component or a generative text-bio model. The core contribution is a tokenization method for genomic sequences.","evidence_snippet":"“systematically benchmark k-mer and Byte-Pair Encoding (