omponent_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","generative_model_present":"yes","foundation_model_evidence":"unclear"}}},{"record_id":"rec_003213","title":"ChIP-GPT: a managed large language model for robust data extraction from biomedical database records","abstract":"Abstract\n               Increasing volumes of biomedical data are amassing in databases. Large-scale analyses of these data have wide-ranging applications in biology and medicine. Such analyses require tools to characterize and process entries at scale. However, existing tools, mainly centered on extracting predefined fields, often fail to comprehensively process database entries or correct evident errors—a task humans can easily perform. These tools also lack the ability to reason like domain experts, hindering their robustness and analytical depth. Recent advances with large language models (LLMs) provide a fundamentally new way to query databases. But while a tool such as ChatGPT is adept at answering questions about manually input records, challenges arise when scaling up this process. First, interactions with the LLM need to be automated. Second, limitations on input length may require a record pruning or summarization pre-processing step. Third, to behave reliably as desired, the LLM needs either well-designed, short, ‘few-shot’ examples, or fine-tuning based on a larger set of well-curated examples. Here, we report ChIP-GPT, based on fine-tuning of the generative pre-trained transformer (GPT) model Llama and on a program prompting the model iteratively and handling its generation of answer text. This model is designed to extract metadata from the Sequence Read Archive, emphasizing the identification of chromatin immunoprecipitation (ChIP) targets and cell lines. When trained with 100 examples, ChIP-GPT demonstrates 90–94% accuracy. Notably, it can seamlessly extract data from records with typos or absent field labels. Our proposed method is easily adaptable to customized questions and different databases.","doi":"","year":"2024","venue":"Briefings in bioinformatics","sources":["google_scholar"],"first_pass_outputs":{"scope_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_003213","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"yes","primary_exclusion_code":"none","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"The abstract describes ChIP-GPT as a fine-tuned GPT/Llama system for extracting metadata from biomedical records. It directly links language-model generation to biological database records and ChIP target/cell-line extraction.","evidence_snippet":"\"fine-tuning of the generative pre-trained transformer (GPT) model Llama\"; \"designed to extract metadata from the Sequence Read Archive\""},"architecture_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_003213","paper_type":"application_wrapper","generative_model_present":"yes","foundation_model_evidence":"yes","primary_exclusion_code":"application_wrapper","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"ChIP-GPT is a task-specific system built by fine-tuning Llama and iterative prompting for record extraction. The abstract explicitly grounds it in a pretrained LLM foundation model.","evidence_snippet":"\"fine-tuning of the generative pre-trained transformer (GPT) model Llama\" and \"iteratively\" prompting for extraction."},"python_gate":{"provisional_decision":"ADJUDICATE","provisional_code":"application_wrapper","adjudication_reason":"criterion_unclear_or_reviewer_conflict","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"yes","generative_model_present":"yes","foundation_model_evidence":"yes"}}},{"record_id":"rec_003215","title":"Qwendy: Gene regulatory network inference enhanced by large language model and transformer","abstract":", that uses single-cell gene expression data measured at four  [34] provided GPT-4 with  potential transcription factors and  Generative modeling of single-