./data/screening_codex_full_2026-07-06/role_logs/adjudicator/batch_0079.stderr.log:66:[{"record_id":"rec_001569","title":"The risks of cholera importation and the forecasting system in epidemiological surveillance in the RF","abstract":"The purpose of the study – to determine the effectiveness of proactive epidemiological surveillance of cholera on the territory of the Russian Federation in 2023-2024. \nMaterials and methods. A risk-based approach based on the systematization of official information on the global incidence of cholera was used: Weekly Epidemiological Record of WHO; official and regional websites of WHO; Internet resources: ProMED-mail post; ECDC and other sources. The work was guided by the administrative documents of the Federal Service for Supervision of Consumer Rights Protection and Human Welfare in 2023-2024, and used materials from the operational epidemiological analysis of the reference centre for cholera monitoring on the territory of the Russian Federation. Genome-wide sequencing and genome assembly were performed on the MiSeq (Illumina) platform, genetic determinants were identified in WGSs using the BioEdit 7.2.5 and BLASTN 2.2.29 programs. Statistical processing of the obtained results was performed using a computer program (StatSoft STATISTICA 6.1.478 Russian). \nResults. The analysis of an array of data on the incidence of cholera in the world is carried out, a realistic forecast of the probability of cholera importation risks to the Russian Federation in 2023 is given. The anti-epidemic readiness of the bodies and organizations of Rospotrebnadzor and the Ministry of Health of the Russian Federation was checked, an operational response was carried out to two cases of cholera importation to the Tambov region and the city of Moscow from India in 2023 with their timely decoding, including genomic and phylogenetic characteristics of pathogens. Foci of infection have been localized and eliminated, and its spread was prevented. Cholera was monitored on the territory of the Russian Federation in 2024. Measures were taken to minimize the risks of importation and spread of this infection. \nConclusion. Based on the analysis of the monitoring results for 2023-2024, the effectiveness of the system of proactive epidemiological surveillance based on early detection of cholera importation risks or their precursors, on forecasting, on prompt response in case of cholera epidemic complications and on control of facilities and factors contributing to the possible spread of infection has been proved. The stability of the epidemiological situation of cholera in the Russian Federation has been ensured.","doi":"10.17816/eid642149","year":"2025","venue":"Epidemiology and Infectious Diseases","sources":["semantic_scholar"],"first_pass_outputs":{"scope_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_001569","paper_type":"unclear","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"EC2_no_text_component","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"This is an epidemiologic surveillance study with WGS and forecasting, not a text-bio model paper. No substantive language or text component is described.","evidence_snippet":"“forecasting system in epidemiological surveillance”; “Genome-wide sequencing and genome assembly were performed”"},"architecture_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_001569","paper_type":"unclear","generative_model_present":"no","foundation_model_evidence":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"EC3_not_generative","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"The abstract describes forecasting and surveillance analysis, not a generative architecture. No broad pretraining or foundation-model-like transfer is described.","evidence_snippet":"\"a realistic forecast of the probability of cholera importation risks\"; \"forecasting system in epidemiological surveillance\""},"python_gate":{"provisional_decision":"ADJUDICATE","provisional_code":"EC2_no_text_component","adjudication_reason":"criterion_unclear_or_reviewer_conflict","paper_type":"unclear","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","generative_model_present":"no","foundation_model_evidence":"no"}}},{"record_id":"rec_001594","title":"Intuitive Human–Artificial Intelligence Theranostic Complementarity","abstract":"Deep learning artificial intelligence (AI) algorithms are poised to subsume diagnostic imaging specialists in radiology and nuclear medicine, where radiomics can consistently outperform human analysis and reporting capability, and do it faster, with greater accuracy and reliability. However, claims made for generative AI in respect of decision-making in the clinical practice of theranostic nuclear medicine are highly contentious. Statistical computer algorithms cannot emulate human emotion, reason, instinct, intuition, or empathy. AI simulates intelligence without possessing it. AI has no understanding of the meaning of its outputs. The unique statistical probability attributes of large language models of AI must be complemented by the innate human intuitive capabilities of nuclear physicians who accept the responsibility and accountability for direct clinical care of each individual patient referred for theranostic management of specified cancers. Complementarity envisions synergistic engagement with AI radiomics, genomics, radiobiology, dosimetry, and data collation from multidimensional sources, including the electronic medical record, to enable the nuclear physician to spend informed face time with their patient. Together with physician discernment, application of the technical insights from AI will facilitate optimal formulation of a personalized precision theranostic strategy for empathic, efficacious, targeted treatment of the patient with cancer in accordance with their wishes.","doi":"10.1089/cbr.2025.0021","year":"2025","venue":"Cancer Biotherapy and Radiopharmaceuticals","sources":["semantic_scholar"],"first_pass_outputs":{"scope_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_001594","paper_type":"review_editorial","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"unclear","primary_exclusion_code":"review_editorial","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"This is an editorial/opinion piece discussing generative AI, large language models, and theranostic nuclear medicine rather than presenting a new model. The model content is commentary, not a primary candidate model.","evidence_snippet":"\"claims made for generative AI... are highly contentious\"; \"large language models\""},"architecture_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_001594","paper_type":"review_editorial","generative_model_present":"no","foundation_model_evidence":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"review_editorial","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"This is an opinion/commentary piece about generative AI in clinical practice, not a model paper. It does not describe a new generative architecture or foundation model evidence.","evidence_snippet":"\"claims made for generative AI... are highly contentious\""},"python_gate":{"provisional_decision":"ADJUDICATE","provisional_code":"review_editorial","adjudication_reason":"criterion_unclear_or_reviewer_conflict","paper_type":"review_editorial","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"unclear","generative_model_present":"no","foundation_model_evidence":"no"}}},{"record_id":"rec_001600","title":"Correction to: Multimodal deep learning methods enhance genomic prediction of wheat breeding","abstract":"© The Author(s) 2023. Published by Oxford University Press on behalf of The Genetics Society of America. This is an Open Access article distributed under the terms of the Creative Commons Attribution License (https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/), which permits unrestricted reuse, distribution, and reproduction in any medium, provided the original work is properly cited. This is a correction to: Abelardo Montesinos-López, Carolina Rivera, Francisco Pinto, Francisco Piñera, David Gonzalez, Mathew Reynolds, Paulino Pérez-Rodríguez, Huihui Li, Osval A Montesinos-López, Jose Crossa, Multimodal deep learning methods enhance genomic prediction of wheat breeding, G3 Genes|Genomes|Genetics, Volume 13, Issue 5, May 2023, jkad045, https://doi.org/10.1093/g3journal/jkad045","doi":"10.1093/g3journal/jkad081","year":"2023","venue":"G3","sources":["semantic_scholar"],"first_pass_outputs":{"scope_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_001600","paper_type":"review_editorial","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"review_editorial","uncertainty_reason":"paper_type_unclear","decision_rationale":"The record is a correction notice rather than a model paper, and it only cites the corrected genomic prediction article. The abstract does not provide model details for screening.","evidence_snippet":"\"Correction to: Multimodal deep learning methods enhance genomic prediction of wheat breeding\""},"architecture_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_001600","paper_type":"review_editorial","generative_model_present":"no","foundation_model_evidence":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"review_editorial","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"The record is a correction notice, not a model paper. It contains no abstract evidence for a generative model or foundation-model characteristics.","evidence_snippet":"\"This is a correction to: ... Multimodal deep learning methods enhance genomic prediction of wheat breeding\""},"python_gate":{"provisional_decision":"ADJUDICATE","provisional_code":"review_editorial","adjudication_reason":"criterion_unclear_or_reviewer_conflict","paper_type":"review_editorial","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","generative_model_present":"no","foundation_model_evidence":"no"}}},{"record_id":"rec_001605","title":"Decoding Spatial Tissue Architecture: A Scalable Bayesian Topic Model for Multiplexed Imaging Analysis","abstract":"Recent progress in multiplexed tissue imaging is advancing the study of tumor microenvironments to enhance our understanding of treatment response and disease progression. Cellular neighborhood analysis is a popular computational approach for these complex image data. Despite its popularity, there are significant challenges, including high computational demands that limit feasibility for largescale applications and the lack of a principled strategy for integrative analysis across images. This absence hampers the precise and consistent identification of spatial features and tracking of their dynamics over disease progression. To overcome these challenges, we introduce SpatialTopic, a spatial topic model designed to decode high-level spatial architecture across multiplexed tissue images. SpatialTopic integrates both cell type and spatial information within a topic modelling framework, originally developed for natural language processing and adapted for computer vision. Spatial information is incorporated into the flexible design of documents, representing densely overlapping regions in images. We employ an efficient collapsed Gibbs sampling algorithm for model inference. We benchmarked the performance against five state-of-the-art algorithms through various case studies using different single-cell spatial transcriptomic and proteomic imaging platforms across different tissue types. We show that SpatialTopic is highly scalable on large-scale image datasets with millions of cells, along with high precision and interpretability. Our findings demonstrate that SpatialTopic consistently identifies biologically and clinically significant spatial “topics” such as tertiary lymphoid structures (TLSs) and tracks dynamic changes in spatial features over disease progression. Its computational efficiency and broad applicability across various molecular imaging platforms will enhance the analysis of large-scale tissue imaging datasets.","doi":"10.1101/2024.10.08.617293","year":"2024","venue":"bioRxiv","sources":["semantic_scholar"],"first_pass_outputs":{"scope_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_001605","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"EC2_no_text_component","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"SpatialTopic models multiplexed tissue images with cell type and spatial information. The NLP-inspired topic-model framework is used on images, but no substantive text or language component is described.","evidence_snippet":"\"topic modelling framework, originally developed for natural language processing and adapted for computer vision\""},"architecture_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_001605","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","generative_model_present":"yes","foundation_model_evidence":"unclear","primary_exclusion_code":"none","uncertainty_reason":"foundation_model_evidence_unclear","decision_rationale":"SpatialTopic is explicitly framed as a topic model, which is a generative architecture. The abstract shows scalability and broad applicability, but not clear foundation-model-style broad pretraining.","evidence_snippet":"“a spatial topic model” with “highly scalable on large-scale image datasets” and “broad applicability across various molecular imaging platforms”"},"python_gate":{"provisional_decision":"ADJUDICATE","provisional_code":"EC2_no_text_component","adjudication_reason":"criterion_unclear_or_reviewer_conflict","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","generative_model_present":"yes","foundation_model_evidence":"unclear"}}},{"record_id":"rec_001608","title":"Deep Learning-Based Diagnosis System for Liver Cancer Through Multimodal Determination for Early Treatment","abstract":"The present research provides an analysis of the development and implementation of a deep learning-based diagnosis system for liver cancer. The provided system was developed using a dataset encompassing 2300 sensor readings, including imaging, clinical, and genomic data. A range of deep learning features was implemented to improve the early detection of liver cancer such as CNN, VGG 16, VGG 19, and Inception V3. The data were divided into two categories: one is for training, and another used for testing. The results of the data analysis have shown that VGG 19 provides the highest diagnostic accuracy of 98.76%, with VGG 16, Inception V3 and CNN displaying the levels of 94.5%, 93.4%, and 90.25%, respectively. The Model performance analysis was also augmented at the application of precision, recall, and F1 score indicators, as well as using AUC-ROC values which highlighted VGG 19 as the most effective algorithm. Another method of evaluation was the application of confusion matrices for plotting the results based on the number of true positive, true negative, false positive, and false negative detections, where all the samples from the dataset were added to each model calculation. The obtained results have shown the importance of multimodal data for the increase of diagnosis accuracy and revealed the promising potential of advanced computation techniques in medical analytics. In such a way, it can be stated that the developed deep learning models contribute to the ability of healthcare specialists in the definition of liver cancer at early stages, which allows timely interventions and facilitates the development of a range of personalized treatment protocols. The application of such algorithms has the potential to reshape liver cancer diagnostics, where major tasks are associated with further improvement and validation of the developed algorithms based on clinical case studies.","doi":"10.1109/GlobalAISummit62156.2024.10947919","year":"2024","venue":"2024 International Conference on Artificial Intelligence and Emerging Technology (Global AI Summit)","sources":["semantic_scholar"],"first_pass_outputs":{"scope_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_001608","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"EC2_no_text_component","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"The system uses imaging, clinical, and genomic data for liver cancer diagnosis with CNN-family models. No text or language component is described.","evidence_snippet":"\"dataset encompassing 2300 sensor readings, including imaging, clinical, and genomic data\""},"architecture_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_001608","paper_type":"application_wrapper","generative_model_present":"no","foundation_model_evidence":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"application_wrapper","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"This is a diagnostic system using standard deep-learning backbones for liver cancer detection. It is a predictive application, not a generative or foundation model.","evidence_snippet":"“a deep learning-based diagnosis system” using “CNN, VGG 16, VGG 19, and Inception V3”"},"python_gate":{"provisional_decision":"ADJUDICATE","provisional_code":"EC2_no_text_component","adjudication_reason":"criterion_unclear_or_reviewer_conflict","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","generative_model_present":"no","foundation_model_evidence":"no"}}},{"record_id":"rec_001609","title":"DNACSE: Enhancing Genomic LLMs with Contrastive Learning for DNA Barcode Identification †","abstract":"DNA barcoding is a powerful tool for exploring biodiversity, and DNA language models have significantly facilitated its construction and identification. However, since DNA barcodes come from a specific region of mitochondrial DNA and there are structural differences between DNA barcodes and reference genomes used to train existing DNA language models, it is difficult to directly apply the existing DNA language models to the DNA barcoding task. To address this, this paper introduce DNACSE (DNA Contrastive Learning for Sequence Embeddings), an unsupervised noise contrastive learning framework designed to fine-tune the DNA language foundation model while enhancing the distribution of the embedding space. The results demonstrate that DNACSE outperforms direct usage of DNA language models in DNA barcoding-related tasks. Specifically, in fine-tuning and linear probe tasks, it achieves accuracy rates of 99.17% and 98.31%, respectively, surpassing the current state-of-the-art BarcodeBERT by 6.44% and 6.44%. In zero-shot clustering tasks, it raises the Adjusted Mutual Information (AMI) score to 92.25%, an improvement of 8.36%. In addition, zero-shot benchmarking and genomic benchmarking tests are evaluated, indicating that DNACSE enhances the performance of DNA language models in generalized genomic tasks. In summary, DNACSE has demonstrated excellent performance in DNA barcode species classification by making full use of multi-species information and DNA barcode information, providing a feasible way to further explore and protect biodiversity. The code repository is available at https://github.com/Kavicy/DNACSE.","doi":"10.1101/2025.10.27.684901","year":"2025","venue":"bioRxiv","sources":["semantic_scholar"],"first_pass_outputs":{"scope_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_001609","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"yes","primary_exclusion_code":"none","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"Introduces DNACSE as a contrastive learning framework to fine-tune a DNA language foundation model for DNA barcode identification. The biological modality and the language-model bridge are both explicit.","evidence_snippet":"\"fine-tune the DNA language foundation model\"; \"DNA barcode identification\""},"architecture_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_001609","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","generative_model_present":"unclear","foundation_model_evidence":"yes","primary_exclusion_code":"none","uncertainty_reason":"generative_status_unclear","decision_rationale":"The abstract describes fine-tuning a DNA language foundation model with contrastive learning. It does not clearly state a decoder/autoregressive or other explicitly generative mechanism.","evidence_snippet":"\"fine-tune the DNA language foundation model\"; \"unsupervised noise contrastive learning framework\""},"python_gate":{"provisional_decision":"ADJUDICATE","provisional_code":"none","adjudication_reason":"criterion_unclear_or_reviewer_conflict","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"yes","generative_model_present":"unclear","foundation_model_evidence":"yes"}}}]
