/Users/bogdan.didenko/lpnu/review/data/screening_codex_2026-07-06/role_logs/adjudicator/batch_0004.prompt.txt:52:[{"record_id":"rec_000045","title":"AI foundation models in plant biology.","abstract":"Rapid technological progress has enabled plant biologists to accumulate unprecedented volumes of multi-scale, multi-modal data, yet this abundance of data has intensified the challenge of translating complexity into biological understanding. Foundation models (FMs), large-scale artificial intelligence (AI) systems pretrained on millions of sequences, structures, or images and adaptable to diverse tasks are breaking through this barrier. Across plant science, these FMs are already making an impact: genomic FMs decode regulatory grammar, protein FMs enable rational protein engineering, vision FMs score phenotypes at breeding-population scale, single-cell FMs annotate cell types across species, and FM-powered AI agents accelerate knowledge retrieval and automate research workflows. While experimental validation remains indispensable, foundation models empower plant scientists to accelerate scientific discovery.","doi":"10.1111/nph.71395","year":"2026","venue":"New Phytologist","sources":["semantic_scholar"],"first_pass_outputs":{"scope_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_000045","paper_type":"review_editorial","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"review_editorial","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"This is a broad review of foundation models in plant biology, describing existing FM applications across subfields. It is not a primary model paper.","evidence_snippet":"\"AI foundation models in plant biology\"; \"Across plant science, these FMs are already making an impact\""},"architecture_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_000045","paper_type":"review_editorial","generative_model_present":"unclear","foundation_model_evidence":"unclear","primary_exclusion_code":"review_editorial","uncertainty_reason":"generative_status_unclear","decision_rationale":"This is a review article about foundation models in plant biology. It summarizes existing systems rather than introducing a new generative model with its own pretraining evidence.","evidence_snippet":"\"AI foundation models in plant biology\"; \"Across plant science, these FMs are already making an impact\""},"python_gate":{"provisional_decision":"ADJUDICATE","provisional_code":"review_editorial","adjudication_reason":"criterion_unclear_or_reviewer_conflict","paper_type":"review_editorial","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","generative_model_present":"unclear","foundation_model_evidence":"unclear"}}},{"record_id":"rec_000047","title":"Probing, Fusion, and Trustworthiness: A Systematic Evaluation of Foundation Model Representations for Multimodal Cancer Analysis","abstract":"Foundation models (FMs) have emerged as powerful representation extractors for medical data, yet their generalizability to datasets under distribution shift remains underexplored. This work systematically evaluates FM-based representations on a suite of computational pathology tasks across two real-world commercial cohorts, IH-BC and IH-NSCLC, drawn from the licensed in-house (IH) oncology dataset. The analysis focuses on two modalities, whole-slide images and transcriptomic profiles, drawn from the IH multimodal data. We first benchmark unimodal probing performance across five FMs on eight downstream classification tasks, and find that image and omics representations carry complementary predictive signals. Then we investigate whether multimodal fusion can yield additional gains over unimodal baselines by comparing three image-omics fusion strategies built on paired representations. The trustworthiness of selected unimodal and multimodal pipelines is further assessed through conformal prediction. Our results show that FM representations achieve competitive performance on out-of-distribution data and that multimodal fusion helps mainly when no single modality dominates the signal. Conformal prediction reveals that in the majority of cases where a point prediction fails, the true diagnosis remains recoverable within the prediction set, reinforcing the value of uncertainty-aware inference for clinical support.","doi":"","year":"2026","venue":"","sources":["semantic_scholar"],"first_pass_outputs":{"scope_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_000047","paper_type":"benchmark_resource","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"benchmark_resource","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"This paper systematically evaluates foundation-model representations and fusion strategies on downstream cancer tasks. It is a benchmarking/evaluation study rather than a new model paper.","evidence_snippet":"\"This work systematically evaluates FM-based representations\"; \"we first benchmark unimodal probing performance\""},"architecture_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_000047","paper_type":"benchmark_resource","generative_model_present":"no","foundation_model_evidence":"unclear","primary_exclusion_code":"benchmark_resource","uncertainty_reason":"foundation_model_evidence_unclear","decision_rationale":"This paper benchmarks representations from existing foundation models on downstream classification and fusion tasks. It does not propose a new generative model or establish foundation-model evidence for a candidate model.","evidence_snippet":"\"systematically evaluates FM-based representations\"; \"benchmark unimodal probing performance\"; \"downstream classification tasks\""},"python_gate":{"provisional_decision":"ADJUDICATE","provisional_code":"benchmark_resource","adjudication_reason":"criterion_unclear_or_reviewer_conflict","paper_type":"benchmark_resource","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","generative_model_present":"no","foundation_model_evidence":"unclear"}}},{"record_id":"rec_000048","title":"Artificial intelligence for child health: current capabilities and the next frontier.","abstract":"Artificial intelligence (AI) is reshaping paediatric healthcare, offering new capabilities across diagnosis, monitoring and treatment personalisation. Modern AI systems integrate multimodal data, including imaging, genomics, electronic health records, wearable sensors, environmental exposures and patient-reported outcomes, to generate insights tailored to children's developmental, physiological and psychosocial needs. Advances in machine learning, deep learning, natural language processing, computer vision and generative models are enabling earlier detection of rare diseases, dynamic risk stratification and personalised care pathways. Emerging technologies such as digital twins simulate individual disease trajectories and treatment responses, reducing reliance on traditional trial designs and supporting anticipatory, precision care.The next frontier of AI in healthcare includes adaptive decision support powered by reinforcement learning and advanced time-series modelling, allowing systems to respond to real-time physiological changes and support complex sequential decisions in areas such as ventilation, insulin dosing, deterioration prediction and medication titration. Parallel progress in remote monitoring and smart sensors is shifting care from hospitals to homes, supporting long-term condition management and reducing avoidable admissions. Causal AI offers further potential by uncovering true cause-and-effect relationships, enabling clinicians to understand why interventions work and explore counterfactual 'what-if' scenarios. Looking further ahead, quantum AI, neuromorphic computing and privacy-preserving federated learning may unlock new computational capabilities, enabling ultra-fast analysis, on-device learning and the secure use of distributed paediatric datasets. Realising this future requires rigorous governance, paediatric-specific validation, safeguards for privacy and autonomy and equitable digital access. When developed responsibly, AI has the potential to augment clinical expertise, reduce health inequalities and transform child health outcomes.","doi":"10.1136/archdischild-2026-330388","year":"2026","venue":"Archives of Disease in Childhood","sources":["semantic_scholar"],"first_pass_outputs":{"scope_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_000048","paper_type":"review_editorial","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"unclear","primary_exclusion_code":"review_editorial","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"The abstract is a broad review of AI in child health, covering current capabilities and future directions. It is not a primary model paper.","evidence_snippet":"\"current capabilities and the next frontier\"; \"Artificial intelligence (AI) is reshaping paediatric healthcare\""},"architecture_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_000048","paper_type":"review_editorial","generative_model_present":"unclear","foundation_model_evidence":"unclear","primary_exclusion_code":"review_editorial","uncertainty_reason":"generative_status_unclear","decision_rationale":"This is a broad review of AI in child health, not a primary model paper. The abstract mentions generative models as part of the field, but does not describe a specific generative foundation model.","evidence_snippet":"\"Artificial intelligence (AI) is reshaping paediatric healthcare\"; \"generative models are enabling earlier detection\""},"python_gate":{"provisional_decision":"ADJUDICATE","provisional_code":"review_editorial","adjudication_reason":"criterion_unclear_or_reviewer_conflict","paper_type":"review_editorial","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"unclear","generative_model_present":"unclear","foundation_model_evidence":"unclear"}}},{"record_id":"rec_000049","title":"Artificial Intelligence in Inherited Epidermolysis Bullosa: Current Evidence, Challenges, and Future Directions","abstract":"Epidermolysis bullosa (EB) comprises a group of rare inherited genodermatoses characterized by fragility and blistering of the skin and mucous membranes, chronic wounding, and significant morbidity including increased risk of squamous cell carcinoma in severe subtypes. Key unmet priorities include reducing diagnostic latency, establishing objective wound monitoring, enabling early detection of malignant transformation within chronic ulcerations, and developing therapies that durably modify disease progression. Artificial intelligence (AI) encompassing machine learning (ML), and deep learning (DL) is increasingly integrated into EB research and clinical practice to address these unmet needs. This structured narrative review synthesises current evidence on AI applications in EB spanning genetic diagnostics, wound assessment, inflammatory endotyping, drug repurposing, and emerging therapeutic technologies, and integrates evidence from registered clinical trials. In genomics, DL-based splicing prediction models and variant prioritisation frameworks accelerate pathogenic variant detection and reduce diagnostic latency. In wound care, convolutional neural networks-based platforms enable automated lesion segmentation and remote monitoring, while multimodal AI models predict healing trajectories and support stratification of wounds by chronicity. Computational transcriptomic analyses have identified candidate repurposing agents by reversing pathogenic gene expression signatures in EB tissue. Emerging convergence of AI with biosensors-integrated wound dressings and three-dimensional bioprinting of genetically corrected skin substitutes represents a transformative future direction. Translational barriers include limited EB-specific training datasets, algorithmic bias across diverse skin phototypes, the interpretability deficit of DL systems, and evolving regulatory frameworks for AI as a medical device. Expansion of internationally interoperable EB disease registries with standardised wound imaging protocols is identified as the single most impactful intervention to accelerate AI adoption. A minimum endpoint set for AI-assisted EB wound assessment, incorporating wound area trajectory, wound type classification, tissue composition, and paired patient-reported pain and itch scores, is proposed to standardise outcome reporting across future studies.","doi":"10.3390/diagnostics16132022","year":"2026","venue":"Diagnostics","sources":["semantic_scholar"],"first_pass_outputs":{"scope_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_000049","paper_type":"review_editorial","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"review_editorial","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"The abstract explicitly says this is a \"structured narrative review\" of AI applications in epidermolysis bullosa. It synthesizes prior evidence rather than presenting a new text-bio model.","evidence_snippet":"\"This structured narrative review synthesises current evidence on AI applications in EB\""},"architecture_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_000049","paper_type":"review_editorial","generative_model_present":"unclear","foundation_model_evidence":"unclear","primary_exclusion_code":"review_editorial","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"This is a structured narrative review of AI applications in epidermolysis bullosa, not a primary model paper. The abstract summarizes existing tools and future directions rather than introducing a candidate generative foundation model.","evidence_snippet":"\"This structured narrative review synthesises current evidence on AI applications in EB\""},"python_gate":{"provisional_decision":"ADJUDICATE","provisional_code":"review_editorial","adjudication_reason":"criterion_unclear_or_reviewer_conflict","paper_type":"review_editorial","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","generative_model_present":"unclear","foundation_model_evidence":"unclear"}}},{"record_id":"rec_000053","title":"Information from coincidences","abstract":"We prove a single algebraic mixed coincidence identity that unifies a broad swath of information-theoretic variational results. For any family of priors $\\{\\pi_i\\}$ and real exponents $\\{ \\alpha_i \\}$, the log of the mixed count $E_{x\\sim\\nu}\\!\\left[\\prod_{i=1}^W \\pi_i^{\\alpha_i}(x)\\right]$ is simultaneously a Boltzmann coincidence weight, an exponential-family normalizer, a maximum-entropy value, and a KL-barycenter optimum. The identity yields a unified derivation of classical cornerstones of information theory: concentration of empirical distributions (Sanov-type decompositions and Gibbs conditioning), hypothesis-testing error exponents (Chernoff information and its multi-way analogue), change-of-measure inequalities (Donsker-Varadhan and PAC-Bayes), and laws governing rare-pattern coincidences (Erdos-Renyi run-length, iterative guesswork, rate-distortion, and birthday thresholds). Each is recovered as a specialization of the same algebraic equality. It strictly generalizes the classical Renyi entropy and divergence variational formulas (one and two priors respectively) to a $W$-prior simplex, and holds for unnormalized and continuum-indexed priors. Among its consequences are an exact multi-prior PAC-Bayes penalty that subtracts an explicit\"coincidence bonus\"from the usual single-prior posterior penalty, and the asymptotic MAP error exponent for $W$-ary hypothesis testing as an edge-restricted simplex optimum. We demonstrate the calculus at scale on two large alphabets encoding richly modeled sequential languages: on language-model next-token predictives where we recover contrastive decoding, and on human genomic regulatory sequence where it separates correlated from diverse prior families along a sliding-window trace.","doi":"","year":"2026","venue":"","sources":["semantic_scholar"],"first_pass_outputs":{"scope_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_000053","paper_type":"unclear","bio_modality_present":"no","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"EC1_no_bio_modality","uncertainty_reason":"paper_type_unclear","decision_rationale":"The abstract presents a mathematical identity and only uses language-model predictives and genomic sequence as demonstrations. It does not clearly describe a primary biological model paper.","evidence_snippet":"\"We prove a single algebraic mixed coincidence identity\" and \"demonstrate the calculus... on language-model next-token predictives\""},"architecture_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_000053","paper_type":"unclear","generative_model_present":"unclear","foundation_model_evidence":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"EC4_no_foundation_model_evidence","uncertainty_reason":"paper_type_unclear","decision_rationale":"The abstract is a theoretical information-theory paper with illustrative applications, not a clear candidate generative foundation model paper. It mentions language-model predictives only as an example, without evidence of a foundation model being introduced or evaluated.","evidence_snippet":"\"We demonstrate the calculus at scale ... on language-model next-token predictives\""},"python_gate":{"provisional_decision":"ADJUDICATE","provisional_code":"EC1_no_bio_modality","adjudication_reason":"criterion_unclear_or_reviewer_conflict","paper_type":"unclear","bio_modality_present":"no","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","generative_model_present":"unclear","foundation_model_evidence":"no"}}},{"record_id":"rec_000056","title":"Analysis of Multi-Omics Data in Breast Cancer Subtype Classification","abstract":"Breast cancer is a heterogeneous disease with diverse molecular subtypes that exhibit distinct clinical outcomes and therapeutic responses. Accurate subtype classification is crucial for diagnosis, treatment stratification, and prognosis. With advances in sequencing technologies, multi-omics data integration has emerged as a promising approach for characterizing the biological complexity of tumor heterogeneity. This study evaluates feature selection strategies, data fusion architectures, and classification algorithms for breast cancer subtype identification using the METABRIC dataset. Three omics modalities, mRNA expression (EXP), copy-number alterations (CNA), and somatic mutations (MUT), were systematically assessed across single-, two-, and three-modality configurations. Feature selection was performed with ElasticNet-regularized cross-validated logistic regression, XGBoost importance ranking, and Binary Particle Swarm Optimization (BPSO) with CatBoost as the fitness evaluator. Fusion was analyzed through direct concatenation and cross-modal Transformer architectures that enrich expression embeddings with contextual information from CNA and MUT modalities. The evaluated classifiers were support vector machine (SVM), multilayer perceptron (MLP), Tabular Prior-Data Fitted Network (TabPFN), and convolutional neural network (CNN). The proposed pipeline achieved performance comparable to state-of-the-art multi-omics models while maintaining interpretability and reproducibility across different fusion and feature selection paradigms. The results underscore the complementary value of multi-omics integration and the importance of pipeline optimization for robust breast cancer subtype classification.","doi":"10.1109/ACCESS.2026.3695279","year":"2026","venue":"IEEE Access","sources":["semantic_scholar"],"first_pass_outputs":{"scope_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_000056","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"EC2_no_text_component","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"This is a primary multi-omics classification study on breast cancer subtype prediction. The abstract describes omics fusion and classifiers, but no substantive text/language component.","evidence_snippet":"\"Three omics modalities, mRNA expression (EXP), copy-number alterations (CNA), and somatic mutations (MUT)\""},"architecture_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_000056","paper_type":"application_wrapper","generative_model_present":"no","foundation_model_evidence":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"application_wrapper","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"This is an applied multi-omics classification pipeline using feature selection, fusion, and classifiers. The abstract does not describe a genuinely generative model or foundation-model evidence.","evidence_snippet":"\"evaluates feature selection strategies, data fusion architectures, and classification algorithms\""},"python_gate":{"provisional_decision":"ADJUDICATE","provisional_code":"EC2_no_text_component","adjudication_reason":"criterion_unclear_or_reviewer_conflict","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","generative_model_present":"no","foundation_model_evidence":"no"}}}]
