/Users/bogdan.didenko/lpnu/review/data/screening_codex_fulltext_2026-07-09/role_logs/adjudicator/batch_0078.prompt.txt:91:[{"record_id":"full_2026-07-06__rec_003578","candidate_id":"full_2026-07-06__rec_003578","source_record_id":"rec_003578","source_corpus":"uncertain","title":"Causal Circuit Tracing Reveals Distinct Computational Architectures in Single-Cell Foundation Models: Inhibitory Dominance, Biological Coherence, and Cross-Model Convergence","abstract":"Motivation: Sparse autoencoders (SAEs) decompose foundation model activations into interpretable features, but causal feature-to-feature interactions across network depth remain unknown for biological foundation models.\n\nResults: We introduce causal circuit tracing-ablating SAE features and measuring downstream responses-and apply it to Geneformer V2-316M and scGPT whole-human across four conditions (96,892 edges, 80,191 forward passes). Both models show ∼ 53% biological coherence and 65-89% inhibitory dominance, invariant to architecture and cell type. scGPT produces stronger effects (mean | d | =1 . 40 vs. 1.05) with more balanced dynamics. Cross-model consensus yields 1,142 conserved domain pairs (10.6 × enrichment, p &lt; 0 . 001 ). Disease-associated domains are 3.59 × more likely to be consensus. Gene-level CRISPRi validation shows 56.4% directional accuracy, confirming co-expression rather than causal encoding.\n\nAvailability and implementation: https://github.com/Biodyn-AI/bio-sae-circuits (Python). Companion SAE atlases: https://github.com/Biodyn-AI/bio-sae .\n\nContact:\n\nkendiukhov@gmail.com\n\nSupplementary information: Supplementary data are available at Bioinformatics online.\n\nKeywords: mechanistic interpretability, sparse autoencoders, single-cell foundation models, causal circuit tracing, Geneformer, scGPT","doi":"10.48550/arXiv.2603.01752","year":"","venue":"","sources":[],"full_text_context":"[abstract: Abstract]\nMotivation: Sparse autoencoders (SAEs) decompose foundation model activations into interpretable features, but causal feature-to-feature interactions across network depth remain unknown for biological foundation models.\n\nResults: We introduce causal circuit tracing-ablating SAE features and measuring downstream responses-and apply it to Geneformer V2-316M and scGPT whole-human across four conditions (96,892 edges, 80,191 forward passes). Both models show ∼ 53% biological coherence and 65-89% inhibitory dominance, invariant to architecture and cell type. scGPT produces stronger effects (mean | d | =1 . 40 vs. 1.05) with more balanced dynamics. Cross-model consensus yields 1,142 conserved domain pairs (10.6 × enrichment, p &lt; 0 . 001 ). Disease-associated domains are 3.59 × more likely to be consensus. Gene-level CRISPRi validation shows 56.4% directional accuracy, confirming co-expression rather than causal encoding.\n\nAvailability and implementation: https://github.com/Biodyn-AI/bio-sae-circuits (Python). Companion SAE atlases: https://github.com/Biodyn-AI/bio-sae .\n\nContact:\n\nkendiukhov@gmail.com\n\nSupplementary information: Supplementary data are available at Bioinformatics online.\n\nKeywords: mechanistic interpretability, sparse autoencoders, single-cell foundation models, causal circuit tracing, Geneformer, scGPT\n\n[introduction: Introduction]\nSparse autoencoders [Sharkey et al., 2022, Cunningham et al., 2023, Bricken et al., 2023] address the superposition hypothesis [Elhage et al., 2022] by decomposing model activations into overcomplete dictionaries of monosemantic features [Olshausen and Field, 1997]. Scaling these methods to large models [Gao et al., 2024, Templeton et al., 2024] has revealed interpretable features in language models; we recently extended this approach to biology.\n\nUnderstanding feature-to-feature causal relationships is critical for mechanistic interpretability [Bereska and Gavves, 2024]. Causal intervention methods-including activation patching [Vig et al., 2020, Meng\n\nWe presented comprehensive SAE feature atlases for Geneformer V2-316M [Theodoris et al., 2023] and scGPT whole-human [Cui et al., 2024]-two single-cell foundation models [Yang et al., 2022, Hao et al., 2024]-in a companion study [Kendiukhov, 2025a], systematically characterizing over 107,000 features across 30 layers. That work established that both models encode rich biological knowledge-pathway membership, protein interactions, functional modules, hierarchical abstraction-but minimal causal regulatory logic. The atlases revealed what features exist and where they are; they did not reveal how features causally interact across network depth. [truncated]\n\n[discussion_conclusion: Discussion]\n### 3.1 Two distinct computational architectures for single-cell biology\n\nThe cross-model comparison reveals qualitatively different computational strategies. Geneformer distributes computation across 4,608 features per layer with a cooperative, dependency-based architecture (80/20 inhibitory/excitatory, mean | d | = 1 . 05 ), while scGPT concentrates information in 2,048 features with more competitive dynamics (65/35 inhibitory/excitatory, mean | d | = 1 . 40 ). Their hub identities also diverge: Geneformer organizes around chromatin and RNA processing, scGPT around mitochondrial electron transport (Figure 10A). These differences likely arise from the interaction of dimensionality ( d =512 vs. d =1 , 152 ) and tokenization (continuous values vs. rank encoding): smaller dimension forces stronger per-feature effects, and continuous values may encourage competitive dynamics as features negotiate how to represent varying expression levels.\n\n### 3.2 Biological coherence as a universal constant\n\nThe convergence of both models on ∼ 53% shared ontology (Figure 8A) is stable across architecture, feature space, and training data, likely reflecting a structural property of biological knowledge organization: given the hierarchical structure of GO, KEGG, Reactome, and STRING, approximately half of causally interacting feature pairs share at least one annotation. [truncated]\n\n[data_representation: scGPT circuit tracing reveals a fundamentally different computational architecture]\nWe applied identical circuit tracing methodology to scGPT whole-human (12 layers, d =512 , 2,048 features per layer), using 200 Tabula Sapiens cells with scGPT's natively multi-tissue SAEs (trained on 3,000 TS cells across immune, kidney, and lung tissues). Source features (30 per layer at L0, L4, L8) were selected by annotation quality from the scGPT feature atlas. All 12 layers have SAEs, enabling comprehensive downstream tracing.\n\nThe scGPT circuit graph contains 31,380 significant causal edges connecting 90 source features to 1,960 unique target features-95.7% of all 2,048 features per layer (Tables 1 and 6). The total computation required only 37.2 minutes on Apple Silicon, reflecting scGPT's smaller dimensionality.\n\n#### 2.8.1 Stronger individual effects, more balanced dynamics\n\nscGPT edges are substantially stronger: mean | d | = 1 . 40 vs. 1.05 for Geneformer K562/K562. 65.2% of scGPT edges exceed | d | &gt; 1 . 0 , compared to 41.4% for Geneformer (Figure 1B). This likely reflects dimensionality: with 2,048 features (vs. 4,608), each scGPT feature carries a larger fraction of the total representation.\n\nscGPT circuits show a more balanced inhibitory/excitatory ratio : 65.5% inhibitory vs. 80.1% for Geneformer (Figure 1C). The 34.5% excitatory fraction indicates more competitive dynamics: features more frequently suppress each other, so removing one releases others from inhibition. Geneformer's 80/20 ratio suggests a more cooperative, dependency-based architecture.\n\n#### 2.8.2 Biological coherence converges across architectures [truncated]\n\n[data_representation: Supplementary Note 7: Cross-Model Consensus Circuit Architecture]\nOf 1,142 cross-model consensus domain pairs (10.6 × enrichment over chance, p &lt; 0 . 001 ), 303 are highconfidence pairs where both Geneformer and scGPT show mean | d | &gt; 1 . 0 . These consensus circuits cluster into several biological themes:\n\nSecretory pathway and membrane biology. The highest-ranked consensus pairs involve Golgi organization and membrane protein insertion (Supplementary Note 5). Additional pairs include ERAD Pathway → Tail-Anchored Membrane Protein Insertion (Geneformer | d | = 1 . 00 , scGPT | d | = 6 . 66 ), connecting ER-associated degradation to membrane protein quality control, and COPII-Coated Vesicle Budding → Regulation of RNA Metabolic Process ( | d | = 1 . 09 , | d | = 3 . 75 ), linking vesicle trafficking to transcriptional regulation.\n\nEnergy metabolism to gene regulation. Multiple consensus pairs connect mitochondrial function to downstream gene expression: NADH Dehydrogenase Assembly → Positive Regulation of TOR Signaling ( | d | = 1 . 17 , | d | = 3 . 72 ) and NADH Dehydrogenase Assembly → Negative Regulation of Cell Differentiation ( | d | = 1 . 00 , | d | = 3 . 28 ). These circuits encode the known dependence of mTOR signaling and cell fate decisions on cellular energy status.\n\nDNA damage response to cell fate. DDR → Negative Regulation of Cell Differentiation (Geneformer | d | = 1 . 02 , scGPT | d | = 3 . 55 ) and DDR → RNAMethylation ( | d | = 1 . 02 , | d | = 2 . 61 ) extend the DDR cascade (main text) to include differentiation arrest and epitranscriptomic regulation as downstream consequences of DNA damage.\n\n[data_representation: Methods]\n### 4.1 Causal circuit tracing algorithm\n\nFor each source layer L src and each source feature f (selected by annotation quality), we perform the following procedure across N cells = 200 cells:\n\n1. Clean forward pass. Register PyTorch hooks at all transformer layer outputs. Run the full forward pass, capturing the clean hidden state h ( ℓ ) clean at every layer ℓ .\n2. Source ablation. Encode the clean hidden state at layer L src through the source SAE: z = SAE enc ( h ( L src ) clean ) . Zero the activation of feature f : z f ← 0 . Decode back: ˆ h = SAE dec ( z ) . Compute the ablation delta: δ = ˆ h -SAE dec ( z original ) . Apply: h ( L src ) abl = h ( L src ) clean + δ .\n3. Ablated downstream pass. Starting from h ( L src ) abl , manually propagate through all subsequent transformer layers, capturing h ( ℓ ) abl at each downstream layer ℓ &gt; L src .\n4. Downstream measurement. At each downstream layer ℓ , encode both clean and ablated hidden states through the downstream SAE:\n\n<!-- formula-not-decoded -->\n\nfor every downstream feature j .\n\n5. Statistical accumulation. Accumulate ∆ ( ℓ ) j across all cells using Welford's online algorithm [Welford, 1962] for numerically stable running mean and variance. This avoids storing per-cell results.\n\nAfter processing all cells, we compute for each source-target pair:\n\n- Cohen's d = ¯ ∆ j /s j [Cohen, 1988] where ¯ ∆ j is the mean delta and s j is the pooled standard deviation.\n- Consistency = fraction of cells where the delta has the same sign as the mean.\n\nAn edge is significant if | d | &gt; 0 . [truncated]","section_evidence":[{"section_type":"abstract","heading":"Abstract","heading_level":6,"line_start":9,"line_end":24,"text":"Motivation: Sparse autoencoders (SAEs) decompose foundation model activations into interpretable features, but causal feature-to-feature interactions across network depth remain unknown for biological foundation models.\n\nResults: We introduce causal circuit tracing-ablating SAE features and measuring downstream responses-and apply it to Geneformer V2-316M and scGPT whole-human across four conditions (96,892 edges, 80,191 forward passes). Both models show ∼ 53% biological coherence and 65-89% inhibitory dominance, invariant to architecture and cell type. scGPT produces stronger effects (mean | d | =1 . 40 vs. 1.05) with more balanced dynamics. Cross-model consensus yields 1,142 conserved domain pairs (10.6 × enrichment, p &lt; 0 . 001 ). Disease-associated domains are 3.59 × more likely to be consensus. Gene-level CRISPRi validation shows 56.4% directional accuracy, confirming co-expression rather than causal encoding.\n\nAvailability and implementation: https://github.com/Biodyn-AI/bio-sae-circuits (Python). Companion SAE atlases: https://github.com/Biodyn-AI/bio-sae .\n\nContact:\n\nkendiukhov@gmail.com\n\nSupplementary information: Supplementary data are available at Bioinformatics online.\n\nKeywords: mechanistic interpretability, sparse autoencoders, single-cell foundation models, causal circuit tracing, Geneformer, scGPT","original_chars":1337},{"section_type":"introduction","heading":"Introduction","heading_level":2,"line_start":25,"line_end":36,"text":"Sparse autoencoders [Sharkey et al., 2022, Cunningham et al., 2023, Bricken et al., 2023] address the superposition hypothesis [Elhage et al., 2022] by decomposing model activations into overcomplete dictionaries of monosemantic features [Olshausen and Field, 1997]. Scaling these methods to large models [Gao et al., 2024, Templeton et al., 2024] has revealed interpretable features in language models; we recently extended this approach to biology.\n\nUnderstanding feature-to-feature causal relationships is critical for mechanistic interpretability [Bereska and Gavves, 2024]. Causal intervention methods-including activation patching [Vig et al., 2020, Meng\n\nWe presented comprehensive SAE feature atlases for Geneformer V2-316M [Theodoris et al., 2023] and scGPT whole-human [Cui et al., 2024]-two single-cell foundation models [Yang et al., 2022, Hao et al., 2024]-in a companion study [Kendiukhov, 2025a], systematically characterizing over 107,000 features across 30 layers. That work established that both models encode rich biological knowledge-pathway membership, protein interactions, functional modules, hierarchical abstraction-but minimal causal regulatory logic. The atlases revealed what features exist and where they are; they did not reveal how features causally interact across network depth. [truncated]","original_chars":2961},{"section_type":"discussion_conclusion","heading":"Discussion","heading_level":2,"line_start":400,"line_end":454,"text":"### 3.1 Two distinct computational architectures for single-cell biology\n\nThe cross-model comparison reveals qualitatively different computational strategies. Geneformer distributes computation across 4,608 features per layer with a cooperative, dependency-based architecture (80/20 inhibitory/excitatory, mean | d | = 1 . 05 ), while scGPT concentrates information in 2,048 features with more competitive dynamics (65/35 inhibitory/excitatory, mean | d | = 1 . 40 ). Their hub identities also diverge: Geneformer organizes around chromatin and RNA processing, scGPT around mitochondrial electron transport (Figure 10A). These differences likely arise from the interaction of dimensionality ( d =512 vs. d =1 , 152 ) and tokenization (continuous values vs. rank encoding): smaller dimension forces stronger per-feature effects, and continuous values may encourage competitive dynamics as features negotiate how to represent varying expression levels.\n\n### 3.2 Biological coherence as a universal constant\n\nThe convergence of both models on ∼ 53% shared ontology (Figure 8A) is stable across architecture, feature space, and training data, likely reflecting a structural property of biological knowledge organization: given the hierarchical structure of GO, KEGG, Reactome, and STRING, approximately half of causally interacting feature pairs share at least one annotation. [truncated]","original_chars":11406},{"section_type":"data_representation","heading":"scGPT circuit tracing reveals a fundamentally different computational architecture","heading_level":3,"line_start":221,"line_end":291,"text":"We applied identical circuit tracing methodology to scGPT whole-human (12 layers, d =512 , 2,048 features per layer), using 200 Tabula Sapiens cells with scGPT's natively multi-tissue SAEs (trained on 3,000 TS cells across immune, ki