 from multiple species, tissues, and experimental conditions. This extensive training enables UCE to learn universal patterns of gene expression that potentially transcend species boundaries.","original_chars":1442},{"section_type":"data_representation","heading":"Embedding Methods for scRNA-Seq Analysis","heading_level":4,"line_start":317,"line_end":336,"text":"To evaluate cross-species generalization capabilities, we compare five distinct approaches to single-cell representation learning that span traditional dimensionality reduction, established deep learning methods, and recent foundation models:\n\n- PCA provides a linear dimensionality reduction baseline that identifies directions of maximum variance in the data. While computationally efficient and interpretable, PCA is limited by its linear nature and may not capture complex non-linear relationships present in biological data [10].\n- scVI (Single-cell Variational Inference) uses variational autoencoders to learn latent representations of scRNA-seq data while explicitly modeling technical sources of variation such as batch effects and library size differences [11]. While effective for batch correction and data integration, scVI requires retraining for each new dataset and does not capture crossdataset relationships.\n- Geneformer treats cells as sequences of gene tokens ranked by expression level and uses transformer architectures to learn gene regulatory relationships [4]. The model captures important aspects of gene regulation and cell type relationships but was trained on human scRNA-seq data (29.9 million transcriptomes), raising questions about its applicability to evolutionarily distant organisms.\n- scGPT applies transformer architectures to single-cell multi-omics data, treating genes as tokens in a sequence and learning contextual relationships between co-expressed genes [5]. [truncated]","original_chars":3104},{"section_type":"data_representation","heading":"Representation Learning","heading_level":4,"line_start":285,"line_end":292,"text":"Representation learning seeks to discover meaningful low-dimensional representations of high-dimensional data that preserve important relationships while reducing noise and computational complexity [21]. In the context of scRNA-seq data, effective representations should capture biological relationships such as cell type similarities, developmental trajectories, and responses to perturbations while removing technical artifacts.\n\nTraditional linear methods like Principal Component Analysis (PCA) identify directions of maximum variance in the data. While computationally efficient and interpretable, PCA is limited by its linear nature and may not capture complex non-linear relationships present in biological data [10].\n\nNeural network approaches can learn non-linear representations through multiple layers of transformations [40]. Autoencoders, which learn to compress and reconstruct input data, have proven particularly effective for biological applications [41].","original_chars":972}],"docling_markdown":"data/docling_uncertain_problem_retry_no_vlm_2026-07-08/markdown/rec_003349_repozitorij.md","first_pass_outputs":{"scope_reviewer":{"record_id":"full_2026-07-06__rec_003349","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"EC2_no_text_component","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"This is a primary empirical study on scRNA-seq foundation models in Dictyostelium, not a review, resource, or wrapper. The candidate modeling is biological only: it uses gene/protein sequences and gene tokens, with no natural-language text component or text-bio bridge.","evidence_snippet":"\"UCE\" uses \"protein embeddings generated by ESM2\" and \"Geneformer treats cells as sequences of gene tokens\"; no natural-language text is described in the candidate model sections. \",\"evidence_for_text_component\":\"none\",\"evidence_for_text_bio_bridge\":\"none\",\"evidence_for_generative_model\":\"not_assessed\",\"boundary_case\":\"biological_token_only_boundary\"}]}}#!/bin/sh# End of File## End Patch## End of File### End Patch"},"architecture_reviewer":{"record_id":"full_2026-07-06__rec_003349","paper_type":"application_wrapper","generative_model_present":"no","foundation_model_evidence":"yes","primary_exclusion_code":"application_wrapper","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"The thesis evaluates existing foundation models (Geneformer, scGPT, UCE) on cross-species cell profiling rather than presenting a new model. UCE uses a masked gene prediction objective and large-scale pretraining, but the candidate is described as a predictive representation learner, not a clearly generative architecture.","evidence_snippet":"“We systematically compared ... foundation models, including Geneformer, scGPT, and Universal Cell Embedding (UCE).” / “UCE is trained using a self-supervised masked gene prediction objective.” / “over 36 million cells from multiple species”,"},"python_gate":{"provisional_decision":"ADJUDICATE","provisional_code":"EC2_no_text_component","adjudication_reason":"criterion_unclear_or_reviewer_conflict","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","generative_model_present":"no","foundation_model_evidence":"yes"}}}]
