/Users/bogdan.didenko/lpnu/review/data/screening_codex_fulltext_2026-07-09/role_logs/adjudicator/batch_0017.prompt.txt:91:[{"record_id":"full_2026-07-06__rec_001617","candidate_id":"full_2026-07-06__rec_001617","source_record_id":"rec_001617","source_corpus":"include","title":"Cell-o1: Training LLMs to Solve Single-Cell Reasoning Puzzles with Reinforcement Learning","abstract":"Cell type annotation is a key task in analyzing the heterogeneity of single-cell RNA sequencing data. Although recent foundation models automate this process, they typically annotate cells independently, without considering batch-level cellular context or providing explanatory reasoning. In contrast, human experts often annotate distinct cell types for different cell clusters based on their domain knowledge. To mimic this workflow, we introduce the CellPuzzles task, where the objective is to assign unique cell types to a batch of cells. This benchmark spans diverse tissues, diseases, and donor conditions, and requires reasoning across the batch-level cellular context to ensure label uniqueness. We find that off-the-shelf large language models (LLMs) struggle on CellPuzzles , with the best baseline (OpenAI's o1 ) achieving only 19.0% batch-level accuracy. To fill this gap, we propose Cell-o1 , a 7B LLM trained via supervised fine-tuning on distilled reasoning traces, followed by reinforcement learning with batch-level rewards. Cell-o1 achieves state-ofthe-art performance, outperforming o1 by over 73% and generalizing well across contexts. Further analysis of training dynamics and reasoning behaviors provides insights into batch-level annotation performance and emergent expert-like reasoning. Code and data are available at https://github.com/ncbi-nlp/cell-o1 .\n\nFigure 1: Overview of this work. Cell-o1 achieves state-of-the-art on the CellPuzzles task.\n\n<!-- image -->\n\n∗ Equal Contribution.\n\n† Corresponding Author.","doi":"10.48550/arXiv.2506.02911","year":"","venue":"","sources":[],"full_text_context":"[abstract: Abstract]\nCell type annotation is a key task in analyzing the heterogeneity of single-cell RNA sequencing data. Although recent foundation models automate this process, they typically annotate cells independently, without considering batch-level cellular context or providing explanatory reasoning. In contrast, human experts often annotate distinct cell types for different cell clusters based on their domain knowledge. To mimic this workflow, we introduce the CellPuzzles task, where the objective is to assign unique cell types to a batch of cells. This benchmark spans diverse tissues, diseases, and donor conditions, and requires reasoning across the batch-level cellular context to ensure label uniqueness. We find that off-the-shelf large language models (LLMs) struggle on CellPuzzles , with the best baseline (OpenAI's o1 ) achieving only 19.0% batch-level accuracy. To fill this gap, we propose Cell-o1 , a 7B LLM trained via supervised fine-tuning on distilled reasoning traces, followed by reinforcement learning with batch-level rewards. Cell-o1 achieves state-ofthe-art performance, outperforming o1 by over 73% and generalizing well across contexts. Further analysis of training dynamics and reasoning behaviors provides insights into batch-level annotation performance and emergent expert-like reasoning. Code and data are available at https://github.com/ncbi-nlp/cell-o1 .\n\nFigure 1: Overview of this work. Cell-o1 achieves state-of-the-art on the CellPuzzles task.\n\n<!-- image -->\n\n∗ Equal Contribution.\n\n† Corresponding Author.\n\n[introduction: Introduction]\nAssigning accurate cell types to single-cell RNA sequencing (scRNA-seq) profiles is fundamental for understanding biological heterogeneity across tissues, diseases, and individuals [1, 2]. Traditional annotation workflows rely heavily on expert knowledge, typically involving clustering to group similar cells [3, 4], followed by manual inspection of marker gene expression to assign cell type labels based on biological domain knowledge [5, 6]. While accurate, this process is time-consuming and labor-intensive, with limited scalability across large or new datasets [1, 2, 5, 7, 8].\n\nRecent advances in deep learning have significantly improved automated cell type annotation through the development of single-cell foundation models [9-11]. These models leverage large-scale unsupervised pre-training to capture complex gene-expression patterns, enabling better representation learning and enhanced performance across various downstream tasks. In parallel, large language models (LLMs) have also been adapted for single-cell applications, either by translating gene-expression profiles into textual representations [12-15] or by integrating multimodal gene embeddings [16-19]. However, both foundation models and LLMs typically annotate each cell independently without considering the shared biological context or batch-level gene-expression information, which fundamentally differs from expert annotation practices [20]. [truncated]\n\n[discussion_conclusion: Conclusion]\nThis work presents CellPuzzles , a structured reasoning benchmark for batch-level cell type annotation designed to emulate expert workflows. We further introduce Cell-o1 , an LLM that demonstrates emergent expert-like reasoning behaviors-such as self-reflection and curriculum annotation-enabling accurate and interpretable predictions across diverse cellular contexts. Looking ahead, future directions include incorporating retrieval-augmented methods [44-47] to integrate external knowledge, leveraging domain ontologies [48, 49] for more consistent reasoning [50], and extending structured reasoning to broader biomedical applications [51].\n\n#### Acknowledgments\n\nThis research was supported by the Division of Intramural Research (DIR) of the National Library of Medicine (NLM), National Institutes of Health.\n\n#### References\n\n- [1] Vladimir Yu Kiselev, Tallulah S Andrews, and Martin Hemberg. Challenges in unsupervised clustering of single-cell rna-seq data. Nature Reviews Genetics , 20(5):273-282, 2019.\n- [2] Malte D Luecken and Fabian J Theis. Current best practices in single-cell rna-seq analysis: a tutorial. Molecular systems biology , 15(6):e8746, 2019.\n- [3] Andrew Butler, Paul Hoffman, Peter Smibert, Efthymia Papalexi, and Rahul Satija. Integrating single-cell transcriptomic data across different conditions, technologies, and species. Nature biotechnology , 36(5):411-420, 2018.\n- [4] Vincent A Traag, Ludo Waltman, and Nees Jan Van Eck. From louvain to leiden: guaranteeing well-connected communities. [truncated]\n\n[data_representation: Dataset]\n### A.1 Data Sources\n\nOur benchmark is constructed by collecting and curating a diverse range of high-quality single-cell RNA sequencing (scRNA-seq) datasets from publicly available sources, primarily hosted on the CELLxGENE platform [39]. These datasets cover a wide spectrum of human tissues, diseases, and biological conditions, ensuring both diversity and real-world relevance for evaluating model generalization.\n\nTable 4 summarizes all datasets used in our study, along with their corresponding accession links and references. The collection includes data from various immune-related diseases (e.g., rheumatoid arthritis, lupus, HIV), cancer types (e.g., lung adenocarcinoma, ovarian cancer), as well as healthy tissues across developmental and anatomical contexts.\n\nTable 4: Raw scRNA-seq data resources involved in our paper.\n\n| Data Source Name | URL |\n|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Single-cell Human Microglia [52] | https://cellxgene.cziscience.com/collections/ fcb3d1c1-03d2-41ac-8229-458e072b7a1c |\n| Single-Cell RNA-Seq Analysis Reveals Cell Subsets And Gene Signa- tures Associated With Rheumatoid Arthritis Disease Activity [53] | https://cellxgene.cziscience.com/collections/ e1a9ca56-f2ee-435d-980a-4f49ab7a952b |\n| The Single-Cell Lung Cancer Atlas (LuCA) [54] | https://cellxgene.cziscience.com/collections/ edb893ee-4066-4128-9aec-5eb2b03f8287 |\n| [truncated]","section_evidence":[{"section_type":"abstract","heading":"Abstract","heading_level":4,"line_start":5,"line_end":16,"text":"Cell type annotation is a key task in analyzing the heterogeneity of single-cell RNA sequencing data. Although recent foundation models automate this process, they typically annotate cells independently, without considering batch-level cellular context or providing explanatory reasoning. In contrast, human experts often annotate distinct cell types for different cell clusters based on their domain knowledge. To mimic this workflow, we introduce the CellPuzzles task, where the objective is to assign unique cell types to a batch of cells. This benchmark spans diverse tissues, diseases, and donor conditions, and requires reasoning across the batch-level cellular context to ensure label uniqueness. We find that off-the-shelf large language models (LLMs) struggle on CellPuzzles , with the best baseline (OpenAI's o1 ) achieving only 19.0% batch-level accuracy. To fill this gap, we propose Cell-o1 , a 7B LLM trained via supervised fine-tuning on distilled reasoning traces, followed by reinforcement learning with batch-level rewards. Cell-o1 achieves state-ofthe-art performance, outperforming o1 by over 73% and generalizing well across contexts. Further analysis of training dynamics and reasoning behaviors provides insights into batch-level annotation performance and emergent expert-like reasoning. Code and data are available at https://github.com/ncbi-nlp/cell-o1 .\n\nFigure 1: Overview of this work. Cell-o1 achieves state-of-the-art on the CellPuzzles task.\n\n<!-- image -->\n\n∗ Equal Contribution.\n\n† Corresponding Author.","original_chars":1537},{"section_type":"introduction","heading":"Introduction","heading_level":2,"line_start":17,"line_end":30,"text":"Assigning accurate cell types to single-cell RNA sequencing (scRNA-seq) profiles is fundamental for understanding biological heterogeneity across tissues, diseases, and individuals [1, 2]. Traditional annotation workflows rely heavily on expert knowledge, typically involving clustering to group similar cells [3, 4], followed by manual inspection of marker gene expression to assign cell type labels based on biological domain knowledge [5, 6]. While accurate, this process is time-consuming and labor-intensive, with limited scalability across large or new datasets [1, 2, 5, 7, 8].\n\nRecent advances in deep learning have significantly improved automated cell type annotation through the development of single-cell foundation models [9-11]. These models leverage large-scale unsupervised pre-training to capture complex gene-expression patterns, enabling better representation learning and enhanced performance across various downstream tasks. In parallel, large language models (LLMs) have also been adapted for single-cell applications, either by translating gene-expression profiles into textual representations [12-15] or by integrating multimodal gene embeddings [16-19]. However, both foundation models and LLMs typically annotate each cell independently without considering the shared biological context or batch-level gene-expression information, which fundamentally differs from expert annotation practices [20]. [truncated]","original_chars":3871},{"section_type":"discussion_conclusion","heading":"Conclusion","heading_level":2,"line_start":198,"line_end":285,"text":"This work presents CellPuzzles , a structured reasoning benchmark for batch-level cell type annotation designed to emulate expert workflows. We further introduce Cell-o1 , an LLM that demonstrates emergent expert-like reasoning behaviors-such as self-reflection and curriculum annotation-enabling accurate and interpretable predictions across diverse cellular contexts. Looking ahead, future directions include incorporating retrieval-augmented methods [44-47] to integrate external knowledge, leveraging domain ontologies [48, 49] for more consistent reasoning [50], and extending structured reasoning to broader biomedical applications [51].\n\n#### Acknowledgments\n\nThis research was supported by the Division of Intramural Research (DIR) of the National Library of Medicine (NLM), National Institutes of Health.\n\n#### References\n\n- [1] Vladimir Yu Kiselev, Tallulah S Andrews, and Martin Hemberg. Challenges in unsupervised clustering of single-cell rna-seq data. Nature Reviews Genetics , 20(5):273-282, 2019.\n- [2] Malte D Luecken and Fabian J Theis. Current best practices in single-cell rna-seq analysis: a tutorial. Molecular systems biology , 15(6):e8746, 2019.\n- [3] Andrew Butler, Paul Hoffman, Peter Smibert, Efthymia Papalexi, and Rahul Satija. Integrating single-cell transcriptomic data across different conditions, technologies, and species. Nature biotechnology , 36(5):411-420, 2018.\n- [4] Vincent A Traag, Ludo Waltman, and Nees Jan Van Eck. From louvain to leiden: guaranteeing well-connected communities. [truncated]","original_chars":19476},{"section_type":"data_representation","heading":"Dataset","heading_level":2,"line_start":286,"line_end":321,"text":"### A.1 Data Sources\n\nOur benchmark is constructed by collecting and curating a diverse range of high-quality single-cell RNA sequencing (scRNA-seq) datasets from publicly available sources, primarily hosted on the CELLxGENE platform [39]. These datasets cover a wide spectrum of human tissues, diseases, and biological conditions, ensuring both diversity and real-world relevance for evaluating model generalization.\n\nTable 4 summarizes all datasets used in our study, along with their corresponding accession links and references. The collection includes data from various immune-related diseases (e.g., rheumatoid arthritis, lupus, HIV), cancer types (e.g., lung adenocarcinoma, ovarian cancer), as well as healthy tissues across developmental and anatomical contexts.\n\nTable 4: Raw scRNA-seq data resources involved in our paper.\n\n| Data Source Name | URL |\n|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Single-cell Human Microglia [52] | https://cellxgene.cziscience.com/collections/ fcb3d1c1-03d2-41ac-8229-458e072b7a1c |\n| Single-Cell RNA-Seq Analysis Reveals Cell Subsets And Gene Signa- tures Associated With Rheumatoid Arthritis Disease Activity [53] | https://cellxgene.cziscience.com/collections/ e1a9ca56-f2ee-435d-980a-4f49ab7a952b |\n| The Single-Cell Lung Cancer Atlas (LuCA) [54] | https://cellxgene.cziscience.com/collections/ edb893ee-4066-4128-9aec-5eb2b03f8287 |\n| [truncated]","original_chars":4332}],"docling_markdown":"data/docling_include_no_vlm_2026-07-09/markdown/full_2026_07_06_rec_001617.md","first_pass_outputs":{"scope_reviewer":{"record_id":"full_2026-07-06__rec_001617","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"yes","primary_exclusion_code":"none","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"This is a primary model paper introducing Cell-o1, a 7B LLM for batch-level cell type annotation on scRNA-seq data. The abstract supports both a language-model component and a direct text-bio task bridge rather than a benchmark-only or wrapper paper.","evidence_snippet":"\"we propose Cell-o1, a 7B LLM\"; \"cell type annotation ... single-cell RNA sequencing data\"; \"distilled reasoning traces\""},"architecture_reviewer":{"record_id":"full_2026-07-06__rec_001617","paper_type":"application_wrapper","generative_model_present":"yes","foundation_model_evidence":"unclear","primary_exclusion_code":"application_wrapper","uncertainty_reason":"mixed_signals","decision_rationale":"The paper proposes Cell-o1 as a 7B LLM trained with supervised fine-tuning and reinforcement learning for a specific CellPuzzles annotation task, which makes it generative but task-wrapped. The abstract gives only limited foundation-model-like evidence beyond broad context generalization.","evidence_snippet":"\"a 7B LLM trained via supervised fine-tuning ... followed by reinforcement learning\"; \"generalizing well across contexts\""},"python_gate":{"provisional_decision":"ADJUDICATE","provisional_code":"application_wrapper","adjudication_reason":"criterion_unclear_or_reviewer_conflict","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"yes","generative_model_present":"yes","foundation_model_evidence":"unclear"}}}]
