igned for effective joint reasoning.\n\n### 3.2 TWO-STAGE TRAINING\n\nAlignment Although LLM can, in principle, learn cross-space attention through sufficient instruction tuning, pre-aligning the bio and language embeddings provides a strong inductive prior: it reduces task-specific tuning burden and improves zero/few-shot generalization to unseen tasks. To achieve this, we introduce a CLIP-style alignment stage using paired data ( x b , t b ) , where the projection P θ is trained so that the bio embedding z b = f b ( x b ) is close to its language counterpart ϕ ( t b ) , which represents the text's embedding.\n\nIn the base training mode illustrated in Figure 1, all data is processed through LLM's forward pass, and a standard autoregressive cross-entropy loss (with teacher forcing) is used to guide learning. [truncated]","original_chars":7872},{"section_type":"data_representation","heading":"BIOVERSE: REPRESENTATION ALIGNMENT OF BIOMEDICAL MODALITIES TO LLMS FOR MULTIMODAL REASONING","heading_level":2,"line_start":1,"line_end":12,"text":"Ching-Huei Tsou 1 ∗ Michal Ozery-Flato 2 ∗ Ella Barkan 2 Diwakar Mahajan 1 Ben Shapira 2\n\n1 IBM T.J. Watson Research Center, USA 2 IBM Research, Haifa, Israel\n\n{ ctsou, dmahaja } @us.ibm.com { ozery, ella } @il.ibm.com ben.shapira@ibm.com\n\n### ABSTRACT\n\nRecent advances in large language models (LLMs) and biomedical foundation models (BioFMs) have achieved strong results in biological text reasoning, molecular modeling, and single-cell analysis, yet they remain siloed in disjoint embedding spaces, limiting cross-modal reasoning. We present BIOVERSE ( Bio medical V ector E mbedding R ealignment for S emantic E ngagement), a two-stage approach that adapts pretrained BioFMs as modality encoders and aligns them with LLMs through lightweight, modality-specific projection layers. The approach first aligns each modality to a shared LLM space through independently trained projections, allowing them to interoperate naturally, and then applies standard instruction tuning with multi-modal data to bring them together for downstream reasoning. By unifying raw biomedical data with knowledge embedded in LLMs, the approach enables zero-shot annotation, cross-modal question answering, and interactive, explainable dialogue. Across tasks spanning cell-type annotation, molecular description, and protein function reasoning, compact BIOVERSE configurations surpass larger LLM baselines while enabling richer, generative outputs than existing BioFMs, establishing a foundation for principled multi-modal biomedical reasoning.","original_chars":1523}],"docling_markdown":"data/docling_include_no_vlm_2026-07-09/markdown/full_2026_07_06_rec_002049.md","first_pass_outputs":{"scope_reviewer":{"record_id":"full_2026-07-06__rec_002049","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"yes","primary_exclusion_code":"none","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"The record presents a new model/framework, BIOVERSE, rather than a review, benchmark, or wrapper. It explicitly combines biological inputs with natural-language queries/descriptions and aligns BioFM embeddings to LLM space for cross-modal reasoning and dialogue.","evidence_snippet":"\"Given a biological input x_b ... and a natural-language context or query q\"; \"paired biological entities ... and textual descriptions\"; \"aligns them with LLMs through lightweight, modality-specific projection layers\"; \"cross-modal question answering, and interactive, explainable dialogue\""},"architecture_reviewer":{"record_id":"full_2026-07-06__rec_002049","paper_type":"application_wrapper","generative_model_present":"yes","foundation_model_evidence":"yes","primary_exclusion_code":"application_wrapper","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"The paper wraps pretrained BioFMs with a projection layer and a frozen LLM decoder for multimodal reasoning, so it is generative but wrapper-like rather than a new standalone generative foundation model. It also explicitly relies on pretrained BioFMs and LLMs for broad cross-modal use.","evidence_snippet":"\"pretrained BioFMs as modality encoders,\" \"frozen decoder,\" and \"standard autoregressive cross-entropy loss\""},"python_gate":{"provisional_decision":"ADJUDICATE","provisional_code":"application_wrapper","adjudication_reason":"criterion_unclear_or_reviewer_conflict","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"yes","generative_model_present":"yes","foundation_model_evidence":"yes"}}}]
