/Users/bogdan.didenko/lpnu/review/data/screening_codex_fulltext_2026-07-09/role_logs/adjudicator/batch_0013.prompt.txt:91:[{"record_id":"full_2026-07-06__rec_001358","candidate_id":"full_2026-07-06__rec_001358","source_record_id":"rec_001358","source_corpus":"include","title":"Harnessing the Power of Single-Cell Large Language Models with Parameter Efficient Fine-Tuning using scPEFT","abstract":"","doi":"10.1038/s42256-025-01170-z","year":"","venue":"","sources":[],"full_text_context":"[data_representation: Supplementary Note 3: Benchmarking Dataset descriptions]\nMS . The MS dataset comprises 21,312 single cells from nine healthy controls and 12 individuals with multiple sclerosis (MS), as annotated in the original study 4 . In alignment with the scGPT data filtering protocol, we excluded B cells, T cells, and oligodendrocyte B cells present only in diseased donors. We selected the top 2,000 highly variable genes per cell for analysis. Cells from 13 random donors were assigned to the reference set, and cells from the remaining 8 donors were assigned to the query set, using a 5-fold cross-validation for performance evaluation.\n\nNSCLC . The NSCLC dataset includes 77,030 cells from 36 patients with NSCLC, categorized by treatment response. This dataset emphasizes the role of T cells, annotated by their subtype in the original study 5 , within the tumor immune microenvironment. For analysis, cells from 28 patients were randomly assigned to the reference set, and cells from the remaining 8 individuals were assigned to the query set, using 5-fold cross-validation. We selected the top 2,000 highly variable genes per cell to conduct the cell type identification.\n\nCOVID-19 . This dataset consists of 77,168 cells from 16 hospitalized COVID-19 patients with varying symptom severities, including seven asymptomatic, eight moderate, and one severe, as documented in the original study 6 . We performed a 5-fold cross-validation, randomly selecting cells from 10 patients as the reference set and the remaining six as the query set. [truncated]","section_evidence":[{"section_type":"data_representation","heading":"Supplementary Note 3: Benchmarking Dataset descriptions","heading_level":6,"line_start":41,"line_end":82,"text":"MS . The MS dataset comprises 21,312 single cells from nine healthy controls and 12 individuals with multiple sclerosis (MS), as annotated in the original study 4 . In alignment with the scGPT data filtering protocol, we excluded B cells, T cells, and oligodendrocyte B cells present only in diseased donors. We selected the top 2,000 highly variable genes per cell for analysis. Cells from 13 random donors were assigned to the reference set, and cells from the remaining 8 donors were assigned to the query set, using a 5-fold cross-validation for performance evaluation.\n\nNSCLC . The NSCLC dataset includes 77,030 cells from 36 patients with NSCLC, categorized by treatment response. This dataset emphasizes the role of T cells, annotated by their subtype in the original study 5 , within the tumor immune microenvironment. For analysis, cells from 28 patients were randomly assigned to the reference set, and cells from the remaining 8 individuals were assigned to the query set, using 5-fold cross-validation. We selected the top 2,000 highly variable genes per cell to conduct the cell type identification.\n\nCOVID-19 . This dataset consists of 77,168 cells from 16 hospitalized COVID-19 patients with varying symptom severities, including seven asymptomatic, eight moderate, and one severe, as documented in the original study 6 . We performed a 5-fold cross-validation, randomly selecting cells from 10 patients as the reference set and the remaining six as the query set. [truncated]","original_chars":10558}],"docling_markdown":"data/docling_include_no_vlm_2026-07-09/markdown/full_2026_07_06_rec_001358.md","first_pass_outputs":{"scope_reviewer":{"record_id":"full_2026-07-06__rec_001358","paper_type":"application_wrapper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"application_wrapper","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"The record describes scPEFT as a parameter-efficient fine-tuning framework for existing single-cell models using gene-expression datasets. No natural-language text component or substantive text-bio bridge is shown, so it is a wrapper rather than a candidate text-bio generative model.","evidence_snippet":"\"The MS dataset comprises 21,312 single cells\"; \"We selected the top 2,000 highly variable genes per cell\"; \"single-cell parameter-efficient fine-tuning (scPEFT) framework\"; \"freezing the backbone model\"; \"updating only the adapter parameters\"; \"single-cell large language models\"; \"single-cell analyses\"; \"single-cell language models\"; \"benchmarking dataset descriptions\"; \"cell type identification\"; \"cross-validation\"; \"COVID-related genes\"; \"single-cell atlases\"; \"gene expression\"; \"no mention of natural-language text, prompts, or descriptions\"; \"biological token only\"; \"existing single-cell LLMs\"; \"fine-tuning framework\"; \"adapter parameters\"; \"gene-expression data\"; \"reference set\"; \"query set\"; \"performance evaluation\"; \"attention-mechanism analysis\"; \"cell states\"; \"blood cell subpopulations\"; \"outperformed zero-shot models and traditional fine-tuning\"; \"resource-constrained researchers\"; \"scLLMs\"; \"limited custom data\"; \"general single-cell analyses\"; \"condition-specific interpretations\"; \"low-dimensional adapters\"; \"adapt specific tasks\"; \"mitigates catastrophic forgetting\"; \"reduces parameter tuning by over 96%\"; \"decreases GPU memory usage by more than half\"; \"undercharacterized cell population tasks\"; \"cross-species\"; \"disease-specific\"; \"out-of-context applications\"; \"zero-shot predictions can be unreliable\"; \"top 2,000 highly variable genes per cell\"; \"healthy controls\"; \"multiple sclerosis\"; \"NSCLC\"; \"COVID-19 patients\"; \"treatment response\"; \"T cells\"; \"tumor immune microenvironment\"; \"symptom severities\"; \"single-cell language models\"; \"frozen backbone model\"; \"limited custom data\"; \"select the top 2,000 highly variable genes per cell to conduct the cell type identification\"; \"using 5-fold cross-validation\"; \"benchmarking dataset descriptions\"; \"single-cell parameter-efficient fine-tuning framework\"; \"single-cell analyses\"; \"resource-constrained researchers\"; \"generalization in out-of-distribution scenarios\""},"architecture_reviewer":{"record_id":"full_2026-07-06__rec_001358","paper_type":"application_wrapper","generative_model_present":"unclear","foundation_model_evidence":"unclear","primary_exclusion_code":"application_wrapper","uncertainty_reason":"generative_status_unclear","decision_rationale":"The title indicates a PEFT method applied to existing single-cell large language models, which fits an application/wrapper pattern. The provided full-text section only shows benchmarking dataset descriptions and does not state a generative architecture or foundation-model properties.","evidence_snippet":"Title: \"Harnessing the Power of Single-Cell Large Language Models with Parameter Efficient Fine-Tuning using scPEFT\"; full text shows only \"Benchmarking Dataset descriptions\" and cell-type identification setup with no generative mechanism described; evidence_for_text_component:not_assessed; evidence_for_text_bio_bridge:not_assessed; evidence_for_generative_model:none; boundary_case:wrapper_boundary"},"python_gate":{"provisional_decision":"ADJUDICATE","provisional_code":"application_wrapper","adjudication_reason":"criterion_unclear_or_reviewer_conflict","paper_type":"application_wrapper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","generative_model_present":"unclear","foundation_model_evidence":"unclear"}}}]
