page_no": 3, "bbox": {"l": 73.131, "t": 112.79841219999958, "r": 86.42512679999999, "b": 105.31448829999954, "coord_origin": "BOTTOMLEFT"}, "charspan": [0, 3]}]}, {"self_ref": "#/texts/182", "parent": {"cref": "#/body"}, "children": [], "content_layer": "body", "meta": null, "label": "text", "prov": [{"page_no": 3, "bbox": {"l": 73.131, "t": 100.84341219999953, "r": 86.42512679999999, "b": 93.3594882999995, "coord_origin": "BOTTOMLEFT"}, "charspan": [0, 3]}]}, {"self_ref": "#/texts/183", "parent": {"cref": "#/body"}, "children": [], "content_layer": "body", "meta": null, "label": "text", "prov": [{"page_no": 3, "bbox": {"l": 73.131, "t": 88.8884121999995, "r": 86.42512679999999, "b": 81.40448829999957, "coord_origin": "BOTTOMLEFT"}, "charspan": [0, 3]}]}, {"self_ref": "#/texts/184", "parent": {"cref": "#/body"}, "children": [], "content_layer": "body", "meta": null, "label": "text", "prov": [{"page_no": 3, "bbox": {"l": 73.131, "t": 76.93341219999957, "r": 86.42512679999999, "b": 69.44948829999953, "coord_origin": "BOTTOMLEFT"}, "charspan": [0, 3]}]}, {"self_ref": "#/texts/185", "parent": {"cref": "#/body"}, "children": [], "content_layer": "body", "meta": null, "label": "text", "prov": [{"page_no": 3, "bbox": {"l": 108.0, "t": 706.6616526, "r": 504.0033873999996, "b": 687.1507743929204, "coord_origin": "BOTTOMLEFT"}, "charspan": [0, 193]}]}], "headings": ["2 RELATED WORK", "2.1 MULTI-MODAL INSTRUCTION TUNING FOR LLMS"], "captions": null, "origin": {"mimetype": "application/pdf", "binary_hash": 7109630141359406345, "filename": "full_text_user_manual_zeromics.pdf", "uri": null}}}
/Users/bogdan.didenko/lpnu/review/data/docling_uncertain_no_vlm_2026-07-08/chunks/full_2026_07_06_rec_003118.jsonl:13:{"candidate_id": "full_2026-07-06__rec_003118", "chunk_index": 13, "text": "LLMshave achieved remarkable breakthroughs across various domains, exemplified by models such as GPT-4 (Achiam et al., 2023). Inspired by them, many transformer-based foundation models such as scBERT (Yang et al., 2022), Geneformer (Theodoris et al., 2023), scGPT (Cui et al., 2024), and scFoundation (Hao et al., 2024) conceptualize SC gene expression as sentences within the language model, merely mimicking LLMs' training strategy to build non-language models for SC data. They pre-train the robust SC embeddings on extensive unlabeled single-cell RNA sequencing (scRNAseq) datasets, followed by supervised fine-tuning across various downstream tasks.", "contextualized_text": "2 RELATED WORK\n2.2 FOUNDATION MODELS FOR SINGLE-CELL ANALYSIS\nLLMshave achieved remarkable breakthroughs across various domains, exemplified by models such as GPT-4 (Achiam et al., 2023). Inspired by them, many transformer-based foundation models such as scBERT (Yang et al., 2022), Geneformer (Theodoris et al., 2023), scGPT (Cui et al., 2024), and scFoundation (Hao et al., 2024) conceptualize SC gene expression as sentences within the language model, merely mimicking LLMs' training strategy to build non-language models for SC data. They pre-train the robust SC embeddings on extensive unlabeled single-cell RNA sequencing (scRNAseq) datasets, followed by supervised fine-tuning across various downstream tasks.", "headings": ["2 RELATED WORK", "2.2 FOUNDATION MODELS FOR SINGLE-CELL ANALYSIS"], "pages": [3], "meta": {"schema_name": "docling_core.transforms.chunker.DocMeta", "version": "1.0.0", "doc_items": [{"self_ref": "#/texts/187", "parent": {"cref": "#/body"}, "children": [], "content_layer": "body", "meta": null, "label": "text", "prov": [{"page_no": 3, "bbox": {"l": 108.0, "t": 650.7446526000001, "r": 504.0033873999997, "b": 576.4397743929208, "coord_origin": "BOTTOMLEFT"}, "charspan": [0, 654]}]}], "headings": ["2 RELATED WORK", "2.2 FOUNDATION MODELS FOR SINGLE-CELL ANALYSIS"], "captions": null, "origin": {"mimetype": "application/pdf", "binary_hash": 7109630141359406345, "filename": "full_text_user_manual_zeromics.pdf", "uri": null}}}
/Users/bogdan.didenko/lpnu/review/data/docling_uncertain_no_vlm_2026-07-08/chunks/full_2026_07_06_rec_003118.jsonl:14:{"candidate_id": "full_2026-07-06__rec_003118", "chunk_index": 14, "text": "However, gene expression data are often affected by the non-biological factors and the information they present is not as stable as text (Du et al., 2019). Therefore, pioneers in SC analysis, such as BioTranslator (Xu et al., 2023) and LangCell (Zhao et al., 2024), try to incorporate text data to make the extracted embeddings more general and robust. Both of them integrates the textual descriptions with biological expression data through pre-trained language models. In particular, LangCell constructs a cell-text dataset utilized to facilitate model pre-training via four tasks grounded in masking and contrastive learning strategies on cell-cell, cell-text pairs, demonstrating initial 'representation' abilities in zero-shot and few-shot scenarios. Another attempt is Cell2Sentence (Levine et al., 2024), which converts each cell into a sentence of gene names ranked by descending expression abundance, and then directly uses the language model for representation. This approach retains only a minimal level of expression data, resulting in insufficient recognition of cell-specific information.", "contextualized_text": "2 RELATED WORK\n2.2 FOUNDATION MODELS FOR SINGLE-CELL ANALYSIS\nHowever, gene expression data are often affected by the non-biological factors and the information they present is not as stable as text (Du et al., 2019). Therefore, pioneers in SC analysis, such as BioTranslator (Xu et al., 2023) and LangCell (Zhao et al., 2024), try to incorporate text data to make the extracted embeddings more general and robust. Both of them integrates the textual descriptions with biological expression data through pre-trained language models. In particular, LangCell constructs a cell-text dataset utilized to facilitate model pre-training via four tasks grounded in masking and contrastive learning strategies on cell-cell, cell-text pairs, demonstrating initial 'representation' abilities in zero-shot and few-shot scenarios. Another attempt is Cell2Sentence (Levine et al., 2024), which converts each cell into a sentence of gene names ranked by descending expression abundance, and then directly uses the language model for representation. This approach retains only a minimal level of expression data, resulting in insufficient recognition of cell-specific information.", "headings": ["2 RELATED WORK", "2.2 FOUNDATION MODELS FOR SINGLE-CELL ANALYSIS"], "pages": [3], "meta": {"schema_name": "docling_core.transforms.chunker.DocMeta", "version": "1.0.0", "doc_items": [{"self_ref": "#/texts/188", "parent": {"cref": "#/body"}, "children": [], "content_layer": "body", "meta": null, "label": "text", "prov": [{"page_no": 3, "bbox": {"l": 108.0, "t": 568.0546526000004, "r": 504.00338739999955, "b": 449.9137743929209, "coord_origin": "BOTTOMLEFT"}, "charspan": [0, 1102]}]}], "headings": ["2 RELATED WORK", "2.2 FOUNDATION MODELS FOR SINGLE-CELL ANALYSIS"], "captions": null, "origin": {"mimetype": "application/pdf", "binary_hash": 7109630141359406345, "filename": "full_text_user_manual_zeromics.pdf", "uri": null}}}
/Users/bogdan.didenko/lpnu/review/data/docling_uncertain_no_vlm_2026-07-08/chunks/full_2026_07_06_rec_003118.jsonl:15:{"candidate_id": "full_2026-07-06__rec_003118", "chunk_index": 15, "text": "Therefore, how to effectively integrate expression data that presents cell-specific information and relatively more stable and consistent text data is still a problem that needs to be explored. In addition, although the embeddings extracted by many foundation models are robust in the zero shot setting, they still require task-specific heads to handle downsteam tasks.", "contextualized_text": "2 RELATED WORK\n2.2 FOUNDATION MODELS FOR SINGLE-CELL ANALYSIS\nTherefore, how to effectively integrate expression data that presents cell-specific information and relatively more stable and consistent text data is still a problem that needs to be explored. In addition, although the embeddings extracted by many foundation models are robust in the zero shot setting, they still require task-specific heads to handle downsteam tasks.", "headings": ["2 RELATED WORK", "2.2 FOUNDATION MODELS FOR SINGLE-CELL ANALYSIS"], "pages": [3], "meta": {"schema_name": "docling_core.transforms.chunker.DocMeta", "version": "1.0.0", "doc_items": [{"self_ref": "#/texts/189", "parent": {"cref": "#/body"}, "children": [], "content_layer": "body", "meta": null, "label": "text", "prov": [{"page_no": 3, "bbox": {"l": 108.0, "t": 441.5296526000006, "r": 504.00338739999955, "b": 400.1007743929209, "coord_origin": "BOTTOMLEFT"}, "charspan": [0, 369]}]}], "headings": ["2 RELATED WORK", "2.2 FOUNDATION MODELS FOR SINGLE-CELL ANALYSIS"], "captions": null, "origin": {"mimetype": "application/pdf", "binary_hash": 7109630141359406345, "filename": "full_text_user_manual_zeromics.pdf", "uri": null}}}
/Users/bogdan.didenko/lpnu/review/data/docling_uncertain_no_vlm_2026-07-08/chunks/full_2026_07_06_rec_003118.jsonl:16:{"candidate_id": "full_2026-07-06__rec_003118", "chunk_index": 16, "text": "In this section, we introduce the novel framework ZerOmics, designed for zero-shot learning for various SC data analysis tasks. As shown in Figure 2, the ZerOmics comprises two principal training stages: (a) pre-training the SC Model to align SC expression profiles with the gene corpus, and (b) multi-modal and multi-task instruction tuning to align SC embeddings with the LLM semantic space. (c) After tuning, ZerOmics achieves zero-shot analysis in various SC downstream tasks without finetuning on labeled SC datasets.", "contextualized_text": "3 METHODOLOGY\nIn this section, we introduce the novel framework ZerOmics, designed for zero-shot learning for various SC data analysis tasks. As shown in Figure 2, the ZerOmics comprises two principal training stages: (a) pre-training the SC Model to align SC expression profiles with the gene corpus, and (b) multi-modal and multi-task instruction tuning to align SC embeddings with the LLM semantic space. (c) After tuning, ZerOmics achieves zero-shot analysis in various SC downstream tasks without finetuning on labeled SC datasets.", "headings": ["3 METHODOLOGY"], "pages": [3], "meta": {"schema_name": "docling_core.transforms.chunker.DocMeta", "version": "1.0.0", "doc_items": [{"self_ref": "#/texts/191", "parent": {"cref": "#/body"}, "children": [], "content_layer": "body", "meta": null, "label": "text", "prov": [{"page_no": 3, "bbox": {"l": 108.0, "t": 357.2426526000006, "r": 504.00338739999955, "b": 293.8967743929209, "coord_origin": "BOTTOMLEFT"}, "charspan": [0, 522]}]}], "headings": ["3 METHODOLOGY"], "captions": null, "origin": {"mimetype": "application/pdf", "binary_hash": 7109630141359406345, "filename": "full_text_user_manual_zeromics.pdf", "uri": null}}}
/Users/bogdan.didenko/lpnu/review/data/docling_uncertain_no_vlm_2026-07-08/chunks/full_2026_07_06_rec_003118.jsonl:17:{"candidate_id": "full_2026-07-06__rec_003118", "chunk_index": 17, "text": "Considering the significant non-biological domain shifts often present in single-cell multi-omics data, and the limitations of SC expression matrices in accurately depicting cellular characteristics, ZerOmics incorporates a multi-modal mask learning paradigm for model pre-training. Our method synchronizes SC expression profiles with gene functions encapsulated in natural language, yielding semantically enriched, robust, and more distinctly characterizable single-cell embeddings, thus enhancing their utility in downstream analytical tasks.", "contextualized_text": "3 METHODOLOGY\n3.1 SINGLE-CELL MODEL PRE-TRAINING\nConsidering the significant non-biological domain shifts often present in single-cell multi-omics data, and the limitations of SC expression matrices in accurately depicting cellular characteristics, ZerOmics incorporates a multi-modal mask learning paradigm for model pre-training. Our method synchronizes SC expression profiles with gene functions encapsulated in natural language, yielding semantically enriched, robust, and more distinctly characterizable single-cell embeddings, thus enhancing their utility in downstream analytical tasks.", "headings": ["3 METHODOLOGY", "3.1 SINGLE-CELL MODEL PRE-TRAINING"], "pages": [3], "meta": {"schema_name": "docling_core.transforms.chunker.DocMeta", "version": "1.0.0", "doc_items": [{"self_ref": "#/texts/193", "parent": {"cref": "#/body"}, "children": [], "content_layer": "body", "meta": null, "label": "text", "prov": [{"page_no": 3, "bbox": {"l": 108.0, "t": 257.49065260000066, "r": 504.00338739999967, "b": 194.1447743929209, "coord_origin": "BOTTOMLEFT"}, "charspan": [0, 544]}]}], "headings": ["3 METHODOLOGY", "3.1 SINGLE-CELL MODEL PRE-TRAINING"], "captions": null, "origin": {"mimetype": "application/pdf", "binary_hash": 7109630141359406345, "filename": "full_text_user_manual_zeromics.pdf", "uri": null}}}
/Users/bogdan.didenko/lpnu/review/data/docling_uncertain_no_vlm_2026-07-08/chunks/full_2026_07_06_rec_003118.jsonl:18:{"candidate_id": "full_2026-07-06__rec_003118", "chunk_index": 18, "text": "Single-cell expression embedding. Single cell expression data often exhibit significant variation, including long-tail effects and domain shifts (Perez et al., 2022). To enhance the robustness of expression embeddings against domain shifts and improve computational efficiency (Yang et al., 2022), the elements in the expression matrix are binned into ˜ X ∈ B = { 0 , 1 , 2 , · · · , N bins } N × G based on expression levels for tokenization, where N bins denotes the number of bins (see Appendix A.3 for details). Additionally, from a biological perspective, this operation emphasizes cell-specific information. Genes that are highly expressed in most cells, such as housekeeping genes, may exhibit lower expression levels in this context. In contrast, genes that are lowly expressed but crucial for identifying cell states, such as transcription factors, may exhibit higher expression levels (Theodoris et al., 2023). To improve model generalization, the random masking (RM) and random substitution (RS) strategy are employed (Kenton & Toutanova, 2019). Due to the sparsity of the expression\n162\n163\n164", "contextualized_text": "3 METHODOLOGY\n3.1 SINGLE-CELL MODEL PRE-TRAINING\nSingle-cell expression embedding. Single cell expression data often exhibit significant variation, including long-tail effects and domain shifts (Perez et al., 2022). To enhance the robustness of expression embeddings against domain shifts and improve computational efficiency (Yang et al., 2022), the elements in the expression matrix are binned into ˜ X ∈ B = { 0 , 1 , 2 , · · · , N bins } N × G based on expression levels for tokenization, where N bins denotes the number of bins (see Appendix A.3 for details). Additionally, from a biological perspective, this operation emphasizes cell-specific information. Genes that are highly expressed in most cells, such as housekeeping genes, may exhibit lower expression levels in this context. In contrast, genes that are lowly expressed but crucial for identifying cell states, such as transcription factors, may exhibit higher expression levels (Theodoris et al., 2023). To improve model generalization, the random masking (RM) and random substitution (RS) strategy are employed (Kenton & Toutanova, 2019). Due to the sparsity of the expression\n162\n163\n164", "headings": ["3 METHODOLOGY", "3.1 SINGLE-CELL MODEL PRE-TRAINING"], "pages": [3, 4], "meta": {"schema_name": "docling_core.transforms.chunker.DocMeta", "version": "1.0.0", "doc_items": [{"self_ref": "#/texts/194", "parent": {"cref": "#/body"}, "children": [], "content_layer": "body", "meta": null, "label": "text", "prov": [{"page_no": 3, "bbox": {"l": 107.99999999999997, "t": 179.99519400000054, "r": 504.00338739999967, "b": 60.07177439292093, "coord_origin": "BOTTOMLEFT"}, "charspan": [0, 1094]}]}, {"self_ref": "#/texts/197", "parent": {"cref": "#/body"}, "children": [], "content_layer": "body", "meta": null, "label": "text", "prov": [{"page_no": 4, "bbox": {"l": 73.131, "t": 710.5574122, "r": 86.42512679999999, "b": 703.0734883, "coord_origin": "BOTTOMLEFT"}, "charspan": [0, 3]}]}, {"self_ref": "#/texts/198", "parent": {"cref": "#/body"}, "children": [], "content_layer": "body", "meta": null, "label": "text", "prov": [{"page_no": 4, "bbox": {"l": 73.131, "t": 698.6024122, "r": 86.42512679999999, "b": 691.1184883, "coord_origin": "BOTTOMLEFT"}, "charspan": [0, 3]}]}, {"self_ref": "#/texts/199", "parent": {"cref": "#/body"}, "children": [], "content_layer": "body", "meta": null, "label": "text", "prov": [{"page_no": 4, "bbox": {"l": 73.131, "t": 686.6464122, "r": 86.42512679999999, "b": 679.1624883, "coord_origin": "BOTTOMLEFT"}, "charspan": [0, 3]}]}], "headings": ["3 METHODOLOGY", "3.1 SINGLE-CELL MODEL PRE-TRAINING"], "captions": null, "origin": {"mimetype": "application/pdf", "binary_hash": 7109630141359406345, "filename": "full_text_user_manual_zeromics.pdf", "uri": null}}}
/Users/bogdan.didenko/lpnu/review/data/docling_uncertain_no_vlm_2026-07-08/chunks/full_2026_07_06_rec_003118.jsonl:19:{"candidate_id": "full_2026-07-06__rec_003118", "chunk_index": 19, "text": "165\n166\n167\n168\n169\n170\n171\n172\n173\n174\n175\n176\n177\n178\n179\n180\n181\n182\n183\n184\n185\n186\n187\n188\n189\n190\n191\n192\n193\n194\n195\n196\n197\n198\n199\n200\n201\n202\n203\n204\n205\n206\n207\n208\n209\n210\n211\n212\n213\n214\n215\nFigure 2: The overall framework of ZerOmics with single-cell data instruction tuning paradigm.\nmatrix, the effectiveness of RM is ensured by sampling the masking matrix M ∈ { 0 , 1 } N × G as\n<!-- formula-not-decoded -->\nwhere 0 < p m < 1 denotes the mask proportion. The RS strategy replaces a certain proportion 0 < p s < 1 of binned values with other values in B . After RM and RS, the matrix ˜ X can be transformed into tokens ˜ X ∗ , then mapped into a learnable embedding space as\n<!-- formula-not-decoded -->\nwhere d represents the dimension of the embeddings and f E is the learnable mapping.", "contextualized_text": "3 METHODOLOGY\n3.1 SINGLE-CELL MODEL PRE-TRAINING\n165\n166\n167\n168\n169\n170\n171\n172\n173\n174\n175\n176\n177\n178\n179\n180\n181\n182\n183\n184\n185\n186\n187\n188\n189\n190\n191\n192\n193\n194\n195\n196\n197\n198\n199\n200\n201\n202\n203\n204\n205\n206\n207\n208\n209\n210\n211\n212\n213\n214\n215\nFigure 2: The overall framework of ZerOmics with single-cell data instruction tuning paradigm.\nmatrix, the effectiveness of RM is ensured by sampling the masking matrix M ∈ { 0 , 1 } N × G as\n<!-- formula-not-decoded -->\nwhere 0 < p m < 1 denotes the mask proportion. The RS strategy replaces a certain proportion 0 < p s < 1 of binned values with other values in B . After RM and RS, the matrix ˜ X can be transformed into tokens ˜ X ∗ , then mapped into a learnable embedding space as\n<!-- formula-not-decoded -->\nwhere d represents the dimension of the embeddings and f E is the learnable mapping.", "headings": ["3 METHODOLOGY", "3.1 SINGLE-CELL MODEL PRE-TRAINING"], "pages": [4], "meta": {"schema_name": "docling_core.transforms.chunker.DocMeta", "version": "1.0.0", "doc_items": [{"self_ref": "#/texts/200", "parent": {"cref": "#/body"}, "children": [], "content_layer": "body", "meta": null, "label": "text", "prov": [{"page_no": 4, "bbox": {"l": 73.131, "t": 674.6914122, "r": 86.42512679999999, "b": 667.2074882999999, "coord_origin": "BOTTOMLEFT"}, "charspan": [0, 3]}]}, {"self_ref": "#/texts/201", "parent": {"cref": "#/body"}, "children": [], "content_layer": "body", "meta": null, "label": "text", "prov": [{"page_no": 4, "bbox": {"l": 73.131, "t": 662.7364121999999, "r": 86.42512679999999, "b": 655.2524882999999, "coord_origin": "BOTTOMLEFT"}, "charspan": [0, 3]}]}, {"self_ref": "#/texts/202", "parent": {"cref": "#/body"}, "children": [], "content_layer": "body", "meta": null, "label": "text", "prov": [{"page_no": 4, "bbox": {"l": 73.131, "t": 650.7814121999999, "r": 86.42512679999999, "b": 643.2974882999998, "coord_origin": "BOTTOMLEFT"}, "charspan": [0, 3]}]}, {"self_ref": "#/texts/203", "parent": {"cref": "#/body"}, "children": [], "content_layer": "body", "meta": null, "label": "text", "prov": [{"page_no": 4, "bbox": {"l": 73.131, "t": 638.8264121999998, "r": 86.42512679999999, "b": 631.3424882999998, "coord_origin": "BOTTOMLEFT"}, "charspan": [0, 3]}]}, {"self_ref": "#/texts/204", "parent": {"cref": "#/body"}, "children": [], "content_layer": "body", "meta": null, "label": "text", "prov": [{"page_no": 4, "bbox": {"l": 73.131, "t": 626.8714121999998, "r": 86.42512679999999, "b": 619.3874882999997, "coord_origin": "BOTTOMLEFT"}, "charspan": [0, 3]}]}, {"self_ref": "#/texts/205", "parent": {"cref": "#/body"}, "children": [], "content_layer": "body", "meta": null, "label": "text", "prov": [{"page_no": 4, "bbox": {"l": 73.131, "t": 614.9154121999998, "r": 86.42512679999999, "b": 607.4314882999997, "coord_origin": "BOTTOMLEFT"}, "charspan": [0, 3]}]}, {"self_ref": "#