/Users/bogdan.didenko/lpnu/review/data/docling_uncertain_no_vlm_2026-07-08/chunks/full_2026_07_06_rec_003118.jsonl:41:{"candidate_id": "full_2026-07-06__rec_003118", "chunk_index": 41, "text": "Table 3: Results of tumor cell discovery. Acc and F1 denote the accuracy and F1-score (as %) respectively.\nCopyKAT, CTC.Acc ↑ = 57.06. CopyKAT, CTC.F1 ↑ = 58.69. CopyKAT, LungCancer.Acc ↑ = 80.16. CopyKAT, LungCancer.F1 ↑ = 50.96. CaSee, CTC.Acc ↑ = 86.67. CaSee, CTC.F1 ↑ = 90.98. CaSee, LungCancer.Acc ↑ = 60.69. CaSee, LungCancer.F1 ↑ = 36.92. ikarus, CTC.Acc ↑ = 89.35. ikarus, CTC.F1 ↑ = 92.11. ikarus, LungCancer.Acc ↑ = 91.45. ikarus, LungCancer.F1 ↑ = 74.85. ZerOmics, CTC.Acc ↑ = 92.81. ZerOmics, CTC.F1 ↑ = 91.53. ZerOmics, LungCancer.Acc ↑ = 92.13. ZerOmics, LungCancer.F1 ↑ = 85.62", "contextualized_text": "4 EXPERIMENTAL RESULTS\n4.2 OVERALL PERFORMANCE COMPARISON (RQ1)\nTable 3: Results of tumor cell discovery. Acc and F1 denote the accuracy and F1-score (as %) respectively.\nCopyKAT, CTC.Acc ↑ = 57.06. CopyKAT, CTC.F1 ↑ = 58.69. CopyKAT, LungCancer.Acc ↑ = 80.16. CopyKAT, LungCancer.F1 ↑ = 50.96. CaSee, CTC.Acc ↑ = 86.67. CaSee, CTC.F1 ↑ = 90.98. CaSee, LungCancer.Acc ↑ = 60.69. CaSee, LungCancer.F1 ↑ = 36.92. ikarus, CTC.Acc ↑ = 89.35. ikarus, CTC.F1 ↑ = 92.11. ikarus, LungCancer.Acc ↑ = 91.45. ikarus, LungCancer.F1 ↑ = 74.85. ZerOmics, CTC.Acc ↑ = 92.81. ZerOmics, CTC.F1 ↑ = 91.53. ZerOmics, LungCancer.Acc ↑ = 92.13. ZerOmics, LungCancer.F1 ↑ = 85.62", "headings": ["4 EXPERIMENTAL RESULTS", "4.2 OVERALL PERFORMANCE COMPARISON (RQ1)"], "pages": [7], "meta": {"schema_name": "docling_core.transforms.chunker.DocMeta", "version": "1.0.0", "doc_items": [{"self_ref": "#/texts/401", "parent": {"cref": "#/body"}, "children": [], "content_layer": "body", "meta": null, "label": "caption", "prov": [{"page_no": 7, "bbox": {"l": 312.743, "t": 535.6546526, "r": 490.94402619999966, "b": 505.18477439292036, "coord_origin": "BOTTOMLEFT"}, "charspan": [0, 106]}]}, {"self_ref": "#/tables/2", "parent": {"cref": "#/body"}, "children": [], "content_layer": "body", "meta": null, "label": "table", "prov": [{"page_no": 7, "bbox": {"l": 313.2848205566406, "t": 495.06048583984375, "r": 489.8094177246094, "b": 432.67950439453125, "coord_origin": "BOTTOMLEFT"}, "charspan": [0, 0]}]}], "headings": ["4 EXPERIMENTAL RESULTS", "4.2 OVERALL PERFORMANCE COMPARISON (RQ1)"], "captions": null, "origin": {"mimetype": "application/pdf", "binary_hash": 7109630141359406345, "filename": "full_text_user_manual_zeromics.pdf", "uri": null}}}
/Users/bogdan.didenko/lpnu/review/data/docling_uncertain_no_vlm_2026-07-08/chunks/full_2026_07_06_rec_003118.jsonl:42:{"candidate_id": "full_2026-07-06__rec_003118", "chunk_index": 42, "text": "Rare cell identification (RCI). We assess the performance of ZerOmics in RCI on two datasets: PBMC68K and 10x scRNA-seq mouse airway epithelium (Airway). Unlike CTA, RCI requires the model's sensitivity to recognize rare cells in the class imbalance scenario. Since fine-tuning models do not support this task, we select three classic supervised methods for evaluation. The results are reported in Table 2. We observe that ZerOmics achieves the best or second-best performance across all metrics. Due to its vast model parameters and training data, ZerOmics exhibits relatively balanced performance across different datasets, avoiding the scenario observed in MARS and scVI where one dataset performs well while another performs poorly.", "contextualized_text": "4 EXPERIMENTAL RESULTS\n4.2 OVERALL PERFORMANCE COMPARISON (RQ1)\nRare cell identification (RCI). We assess the performance of ZerOmics in RCI on two datasets: PBMC68K and 10x scRNA-seq mouse airway epithelium (Airway). Unlike CTA, RCI requires the model's sensitivity to recognize rare cells in the class imbalance scenario. Since fine-tuning models do not support this task, we select three classic supervised methods for evaluation. The results are reported in Table 2. We observe that ZerOmics achieves the best or second-best performance across all metrics. Due to its vast model parameters and training data, ZerOmics exhibits relatively balanced performance across different datasets, avoiding the scenario observed in MARS and scVI where one dataset performs well while another performs poorly.", "headings": ["4 EXPERIMENTAL RESULTS", "4.2 OVERALL PERFORMANCE COMPARISON (RQ1)"], "pages": [7], "meta": {"schema_name": "docling_core.transforms.chunker.DocMeta", "version": "1.0.0", "doc_items": [{"self_ref": "#/texts/402", "parent": {"cref": "#/body"}, "children": [], "content_layer": "body", "meta": null, "label": "text", "prov": [{"page_no": 7, "bbox": {"l": 108.0, "t": 404.824194, "r": 504.0033873999997, "b": 319.17177439292027, "coord_origin": "BOTTOMLEFT"}, "charspan": [0, 736]}]}], "headings": ["4 EXPERIMENTAL RESULTS", "4.2 OVERALL PERFORMANCE COMPARISON (RQ1)"], "captions": null, "origin": {"mimetype": "application/pdf", "binary_hash": 7109630141359406345, "filename": "full_text_user_manual_zeromics.pdf", "uri": null}}}
/Users/bogdan.didenko/lpnu/review/data/docling_uncertain_no_vlm_2026-07-08/chunks/full_2026_07_06_rec_003118.jsonl:43:{"candidate_id": "full_2026-07-06__rec_003118", "chunk_index": 43, "text": "Tumor cell discovery (TCD). We examine the performance of ZerOmics in TCD, a task more closely related to clinical medical research. This experiment involves two datasets: 10x scRNA-seq human circulating tumor cells (CTC) and Lung Cancer (LungCancer). Unlike CTA, TCD requires the model to distinguish cancer cells based on features such as mutations and gene expression heterogeneity inherent in the data itself. Therefore, we select three methods specialized for TCD, which usually model with prior knowledge about tumor expression features (such as CopyKAT, CaSee), or carcinogenic gene markers (e.g., ikarus). The comparison results are presented in Table 3. Among all baseline methods, ikarus achieves a significant advantage, indicating that incorporating carcinogenic gene information can effectively guide the model to recognize cancer. Coincidentally, the gene corpus used to train ZerOmics also includes an amount of natural language text describing the relationships between genes and corresponding diseases.", "contextualized_text": "4 EXPERIMENTAL RESULTS\n4.2 OVERALL PERFORMANCE COMPARISON (RQ1)\nTumor cell discovery (TCD). We examine the performance of ZerOmics in TCD, a task more closely related to clinical medical research. This experiment involves two datasets: 10x scRNA-seq human circulating tumor cells (CTC) and Lung Cancer (LungCancer). Unlike CTA, TCD requires the model to distinguish cancer cells based on features such as mutations and gene expression heterogeneity inherent in the data itself. Therefore, we select three methods specialized for TCD, which usually model with prior knowledge about tumor expression features (such as CopyKAT, CaSee), or carcinogenic gene markers (e.g., ikarus). The comparison results are presented in Table 3. Among all baseline methods, ikarus achieves a significant advantage, indicating that incorporating carcinogenic gene information can effectively guide the model to recognize cancer. Coincidentally, the gene corpus used to train ZerOmics also includes an amount of natural language text describing the relationships between genes and corresponding diseases.", "headings": ["4 EXPERIMENTAL RESULTS", "4.2 OVERALL PERFORMANCE COMPARISON (RQ1)"], "pages": [7], "meta": {"schema_name": "docling_core.transforms.chunker.DocMeta", "version": "1.0.0", "doc_items": [{"self_ref": "#/texts/403", "parent": {"cref": "#/body"}, "children": [], "content_layer": "body", "meta": null, "label": "text", "prov": [{"page_no": 7, "bbox": {"l": 108.0, "t": 298.38119399999994, "r": 504.0033873999998, "b": 157.9337743929202, "coord_origin": "BOTTOMLEFT"}, "charspan": [0, 1229]}]}], "headings": ["4 EXPERIMENTAL RESULTS", "4.2 OVERALL PERFORMANCE COMPARISON (RQ1)"], "captions": null, "origin": {"mimetype": "application/pdf", "binary_hash": 7109630141359406345, "filename": "full_text_user_manual_zeromics.pdf", "uri": null}}}
/Users/bogdan.didenko/lpnu/review/data/docling_uncertain_no_vlm_2026-07-08/chunks/full_2026_07_06_rec_003118.jsonl:44:{"candidate_id": "full_2026-07-06__rec_003118", "chunk_index": 44, "text": "Through the dual-alignment strategy, the LLM can well understand and respond to this information, as evidenced by ZerOmics achieving the best performance in all experiments only except for the F1-score in CTC.", "contextualized_text": "4 EXPERIMENTAL RESULTS\n4.2 OVERALL PERFORMANCE COMPARISON (RQ1)\nThrough the dual-alignment strategy, the LLM can well understand and respond to this information, as evidenced by ZerOmics achieving the best performance in all experiments only except for the F1-score in CTC.", "headings": ["4 EXPERIMENTAL RESULTS", "4.2 OVERALL PERFORMANCE COMPARISON (RQ1)"], "pages": [7], "meta": {"schema_name": "docling_core.transforms.chunker.DocMeta", "version": "1.0.0", "doc_items": [{"self_ref": "#/texts/403", "parent": {"cref": "#/body"}, "children": [], "content_layer": "body", "meta": null, "label": "text", "prov": [{"page_no": 7, "bbox": {"l": 108.0, "t": 298.38119399999994, "r": 504.0033873999998, "b": 157.9337743929202, "coord_origin": "BOTTOMLEFT"}, "charspan": [0, 1229]}]}], "headings": ["4 EXPERIMENTAL RESULTS", "4.2 OVERALL PERFORMANCE COMPARISON (RQ1)"], "captions": null, "origin": {"mimetype": "application/pdf", "binary_hash": 7109630141359406345, "filename": "full_text_user_manual_zeromics.pdf", "uri": null}}}
/Users/bogdan.didenko/lpnu/review/data/docling_uncertain_no_vlm_2026-07-08/chunks/full_2026_07_06_rec_003118.jsonl:45:{"candidate_id": "full_2026-07-06__rec_003118", "chunk_index": 45, "text": "We explore whether ZerOmics, as an instruction-tuned LLM, has the emergent ability to tackle the unseen task. Specifically, we evaluate its performance on the cell pathway inference (CPI), in both zero- and few-shot settings, comparing with the fine-tuned models, geneformer and LangCell-CE. This experiment involves two datasets: 10x scRNA-seq human dilated and hypertrophic cardiomyopathy (HDHC), and liver tissue (Liver). The results are summarized in Table 4.\n378\n379\n380\n381\n382\n383\n384\n385\n386\n387\n388\n389\n390\n391\n392\n393\n394\n395\n396\n397\n398\n399\n400\n401\n402\n403\n404\n405\n406\n407\n408\n409\n410\n411\n412\n413\n414\n415\n416\n417\n418\n419\n420\n421\n422\n423\n424\n425\n426\n427\n428\n429\n430\n431\n", "contextualized_text": "4 EXPERIMENTAL RESULTS\n4.3 CROSS-TASK ZERO-SHOT PERFORMANCE (RQ2)\nWe explore whether ZerOmics, as an instruction-tuned LLM, has the emergent ability to tackle the unseen task. Specifically, we evaluate its performance on the cell pathway inference (CPI), in both zero- and few-shot settings, comparing with the fine-tuned models, geneformer and LangCell-CE. This experiment involves two datasets: 10x scRNA-seq human dilated and hypertrophic cardiomyopathy (HDHC), and liver tissue (Liver). The results are summarized in Table 4.\n378\n379\n380\n381\n382\n383\n384\n385\n386\n387\n388\n389\n390\n391\n392\n393\n394\n395\n396\n397\n398\n399\n400\n401\n402\n403\n404\n405\n406\n407\n408\n409\n410\n411\n412\n413\n414\n415\n416\n417\n418\n419\n420\n421\n422\n423\n424\n425\n426\n427\n428\n429\n430\n431\n", "headings": ["4 EXPERIMENTAL RESULTS", "4.3 CROSS-TASK ZERO-SHOT PERFORMANCE (RQ2)"], "pages": [7, 8], "meta": {"schema_name": "docling_core.transforms.chunker.DocMeta", "version": "1.0.0", "doc_items": [{"self_ref": "#/texts/405", "paren