 {"cref": "#/body"}, "children": [], "content_layer": "body", "meta": null, "label": "text", "prov": [{"page_no": 17, "bbox": {"l": 73.131, "t": 76.93341219999957, "r": 86.42512679999999, "b": 69.44948829999953, "coord_origin": "BOTTOMLEFT"}, "charspan": [0, 3]}]}], "headings": ["B EVALUATION PROTOCOLS", "B.1 EVALUATION TASKS AND METRICS"], "captions": null, "origin": {"mimetype": "application/pdf", "binary_hash": 7109630141359406345, "filename": "full_text_user_manual_zeromics.pdf", "uri": null}}}
/Users/bogdan.didenko/lpnu/review/data/docling_uncertain_no_vlm_2026-07-08/chunks/full_2026_07_06_rec_003118.jsonl:101:{"candidate_id": "full_2026-07-06__rec_003118", "chunk_index": 101, "text": "which one or several of these pathways it expresses. Since the ground truth of the pathway is often not directly provided, we use irGESA (Fan et al., 2024) to analyze the pathways that each cell may express in the evaluation datasets. Note that irGESA is not a predictive model, but a oneto-one matching model for the gene expression pattern of each cell and all known cell pathways. Geneformer and LangCell also need to use the generated labels for supervised fine-tuning, while ZerOmics in the zero-shot setting uses instructions to directly generate answers in a way similar to answering multiple-choice questions. The evaluation datasets are split according to 2:1, and all methods generate results on the test set for comparison. We employ the Area Under the Receiver Operating Characteristic Curve (AUROC) and Area Under the Precision-Recall Curve (AUPRC) for evaluation. Due to the complexity of the predicted labels, these two metrics are also calculated using two different strategies, namely average and flatten.", "contextualized_text": "B EVALUATION PROTOCOLS\nB.1 EVALUATION TASKS AND METRICS\nwhich one or several of these pathways it expresses. Since the ground truth of the pathway is often not directly provided, we use irGESA (Fan et al., 2024) to analyze the pathways that each cell may express in the evaluation datasets. Note that irGESA is not a predictive model, but a oneto-one matching model for the gene expression pattern of each cell and all known cell pathways. Geneformer and LangCell also need to use the generated labels for supervised fine-tuning, while ZerOmics in the zero-shot setting uses instructions to directly generate answers in a way similar to answering multiple-choice questions. The evaluation datasets are split according to 2:1, and all methods generate results on the test set for comparison. We employ the Area Under the Receiver Operating Characteristic Curve (AUROC) and Area Under the Precision-Recall Curve (AUPRC) for evaluation. Due to the complexity of the predicted labels, these two metrics are also calculated using two different strategies, namely average and flatten.", "headings": ["B EVALUATION PROTOCOLS", "B.1 EVALUATION TASKS AND METRICS"], "pages": [17], "meta": {"schema_name": "docling_core.transforms.chunker.DocMeta", "version": "1.0.0", "doc_items": [{"self_ref": "#/texts/963", "parent": {"cref": "#/body"}, "children": [], "content_layer": "body", "meta": null, "label": "text", "prov": [{"page_no": 17, "bbox": {"l": 108.0, "t": 706.6616526, "r": 504.00338739999984, "b": 555.643774392921, "coord_origin": "BOTTOMLEFT"}, "charspan": [0, 1331]}]}], "headings": ["B EVALUATION PROTOCOLS", "B.1 EVALUATION TASKS AND METRICS"], "captions": null, "origin": {"mimetype": "application/pdf", "binary_hash": 7109630141359406345, "filename": "full_text_user_manual_zeromics.pdf", "uri": null}}}
/Users/bogdan.didenko/lpnu/review/data/docling_uncertain_no_vlm_2026-07-08/chunks/full_2026_07_06_rec_003118.jsonl:102:{"candidate_id": "full_2026-07-06__rec_003118", "chunk_index": 102, "text": "Average (a-) metrics treat the 41 prediction results of each cell as independent samples for calculation, and then averages the AUROC or AUPRC results of each cell. While flatten (f-) metrics the 41 prediction results of all n cells as independent samples and calculates AUROC or AUPRC on these 41*n samples.", "contextualized_text": "B EVALUATION PROTOCOLS\nB.1 EVALUATION TASKS AND METRICS\nAverage (a-) metrics treat the 41 prediction results of each cell as independent samples for calculation, and then averages the AUROC or AUPRC results of each cell. While flatten (f-) metrics the 41 prediction results of all n cells as independent samples and calculates AUROC or AUPRC on these 41*n samples.", "headings": ["B EVALUATION PROTOCOLS", "B.1 EVALUATION TASKS AND METRICS"], "pages": [17], "meta": {"schema_name": "docling_core.transforms.chunker.DocMeta", "version": "1.0.0", "doc_items": [{"self_ref": "#/texts/963", "parent": {"cref": "#/body"}, "children": [], "content_layer": "body", "meta": null, "label": "text", "prov": [{"page_no": 17, "bbox": {"l": 108.0, "t": 706.6616526, "r": 504.00338739999984, "b": 555.643774392921, "coord_origin": "BOTTOMLEFT"}, "charspan": [0, 1331]}]}], "headings": ["B EVALUATION PROTOCOLS", "B.1 EVALUATION TASKS AND METRICS"], "captions": null, "origin": {"mimetype": "application/pdf", "binary_hash": 7109630141359406345, "filename": "full_text_user_manual_zeromics.pdf", "uri": null}}}
/Users/bogdan.didenko/lpnu/review/data/docling_uncertain_no_vlm_2026-07-08/chunks/full_2026_07_06_rec_003118.jsonl:103:{"candidate_id": "full_2026-07-06__rec_003118", "chunk_index": 103, "text": "We collect abundant benchmark datasets for evaluating the performance of ZerOmics across diverse tasks. The following introduction of each dataset summarizes the involved cell information and its application scenarios in this paper.\nPBMC68K. The PBMC68K (Zheng et al., 2017) dataset is sourced from a healthy donor, consisting of the gene profiles of 68,450 peripheral blood mononuclear cells (PBMCs). The dataset encompasses eleven distinct cell types, including CD4+ T cells, CD8+ T cells, B cells, natural killer (NK) cells, CD14+ Monocytes, FCGR3A+ Monocytes, dendritic cells, memory cells, helper2 cells, and Megakaryocytes. Cells were processed on the 10x platform using the scRNA-seq technology. This dataset is utilized for the cell type annotation task in this paper. PBMC68K is used for CTA and RCI tasks in this study.", "contextualized_text": "B EVALUATION PROTOCOLS\nB.2 EVALUATION DATASETS\nWe collect abundant benchmark datasets for evaluating the performance of ZerOmics across diverse tasks. The following introduction of each dataset summarizes the involved cell information and its application scenarios in this paper.\nPBMC68K. The PBMC68K (Zheng et al., 2017) dataset is sourced from a healthy donor, consisting of the gene profiles of 68,450 peripheral blood mononuclear cells (PBMCs). The dataset encompasses eleven distinct cell types, including CD4+ T cells, CD8+ T cells, B cells, natural killer (NK) cells, CD14+ Monocytes, FCGR3A+ Monocytes, dendritic cells, memory cells, helper2 cells, and Megakaryocytes. Cells were processed on the 10x platform using the scRNA-seq technology. This dataset is utilized for the cell type annotation task in this paper. PBMC68K is used for CTA and RCI tasks in this study.", "headings": ["B EVALUATION PROTOCOLS", "B.2 EVALUATION DATASETS"], "pages": [17], "meta": {"schema_name": "docling_core.transforms.chunker.DocMeta", "version": "1.0.0", "doc_items": [{"self_ref": "#/texts/965", "parent": {"cref": "#/body"}, "children": [], "content_layer": "body", "meta": null, "label": "text", "prov": [{"page_no": 17, "bbox": {"l": 107.99999999999999, "t": 516.3606526000007, "r": 504.0033873999997, "b": 485.891774392921, "coord_origin": "BOTTOMLEFT"}, "charspan": [0, 232]}]}, {"self_ref": "#/texts/966", "parent": {"cref": "#/body"}, "children": [], "content_layer": "body", "meta": null, "label": "text", "prov": [{"page_no": 17, "bbox": {"l": 107.99999999999999, "t": 469.52419400000065, "r": 504.0037401999995, "b": 394.82977439292097, "coord_origin": "BOTTOMLEFT"}, "charspan": [0, 596]}]}], "headings": ["B EVALUATION PROTOCOLS", "B.2 EVALUATION DATASETS"], "captions": null, "origin": {"mimetype": "application/pdf", "binary_hash": 7109630141359406345, "filename": "full_text_user_manual_zeromics.pdf", "uri": null}}}
/Users/bogdan.didenko/lpnu/review/data/docling_uncertain_no_vlm_2026-07-08/chunks/full_2026_07_06_rec_003118.jsonl:104:{"candidate_id": "full_2026-07-06__rec_003118", "chunk_index": 104, "text": "Pancreas. The Pancreas (Segerstolpe et al., 2016) dataset consists of 2,209 single cells compiled from human pancreatic islets, with samples collected from six healthy and four type 2 diabetes (T2D) donors. The dataset encompasses both endocrine and exocrine cells, a total of eight cell types: alpha, beta, gamma, delta, and epsilon endocrine cells, as well as acinar, ductal, and pancreatic stellate cells (PSCs). The cells were dissociated into single-cell suspensions and sorted using fluorescence-activated cell sorting (FACS), followed by RNA sequencing through the Smart-seq2 protocol. Pancreas is utilized for the CTA task in this study.", "contextualized_text": "B EVALUATION PROTOCOLS\nB.2 EVALUATION DATASETS\nPancreas. The Pancreas (Segerstolpe et al., 2016) dataset consists of 2,209 single cells compiled from human pancreatic islets, with samples collected from six healthy and four type 2 diabetes (T2D) donors. The dataset encompasses both endocrine and exocrine cells, a total of eight cell types: alpha, beta, gamma, delta, and epsilon endocrine cells, as well as acinar, ductal, and pancreatic stellate cells (PSCs). The cells were dissociated into single-cell suspensions and sorted using fluorescence-activated cell sorting (FACS), followed by RNA sequencing through the Smart-seq2 protocol. Pancreas is utilized for the CTA task in this study.", "headings": ["B EVALUATION PROTOCOLS", "B.2 EVALUATION DATASETS"], "pages": [17], "meta": {"schema_name": "docling_core.transforms.chunker.DocMeta", "version": "1.0.0", "doc_items": [{"self_ref": "#/texts/967", "parent": {"cref": "#/body"}, "children": [], "content_layer": "body", "meta": null, "label": "text", "prov": [{"page_no": 17, "bbox": {"l": 107.99999999999999, "t": 378.4631940000006, "r": 504.0035013999992, "b": 303.76877439292093, "coord_origin": "BOTTOMLEFT"}, "charspan": [0, 645]}]}], "headings": ["B EVALUATION PROTOCOLS", "B.2 EVALUATION DATASETS"], "captions": null, "origin": {"mimetype": "application/pdf", "binary_hash": 7109630141359406345, "filename": "full_text_user_manual_zeromics.pdf", "uri": null}}}
/Users/bogdan.didenko/lpnu/review/data/docling_uncertain_no_vlm_2026-07-08/chunks/full_2026_07_06_rec_003118.jsonl:105:{"candidate_id": "full_2026-07-06__rec_003118", "chunk_index": 105, "text": "BMMC. The BMMC dataset referenced in (Granja et al., 2019) consists of 35,882 bone marrow mononuclear cells (BMMCs) collected from healthy donors. The dataset contains six cell types including progenitor cells, B cells, T cells, NK cells, monocytes, and dendritic cells. Cells were profiled on the 10x platform utilizing the single-cell assay for transposase-accessible chromatin using sequencing (scATAC-seq) technology. BMMC is utilized for the CTA task in this study.", "contextualized_text": "B EVALUATION PROTOCOLS\nB.2 EVALUATION DATASETS\nBMMC. The BMMC dataset referenced in (Granja et al., 2019) consists of 35,882 bone marrow mononuclear cells (BMMCs) collected from healthy donors. The dataset contains six cell types including progenitor cells, B cells, T cells, NK cells, monocytes, and dendritic cells. Cells were profiled on the 10x platform utilizing the single-cell assay for transposase-accessible chromatin using sequencing (scATAC-seq) technology. BMMC is utilized for the CTA task in this study.", "headings": ["B EVALUATION PROTOCOLS", "B.2 EVALUATION DATASETS"], "pages": [17], "meta": {"schema_name": "docling_core.transforms.chunker.DocMeta", "version": "1.0.0", "doc_items": [{"self_ref": "#/texts/968", "parent": {"cref": "#/body"}, "children": [], "content_layer": "body", "meta": null, "label": "text", "prov": [{"page_no": 17, "bbox": {"l": 108.0, "t": 287.4011940000006, "r": 504.0036971999996, "b": 234.6257743929209, "coord_origin": "BOTTOMLEFT"}, "charspan": [0, 470]}]}], "headings": ["B EVALUATION PROTOCOLS", "B.2 EVALUATION DATASETS"], "captions": null, "origin": {"mimetype": "application/pdf", "binary_hash": 7109630141359406345, "filename": "full_text_user_manual_zeromics.pdf", "uri": null}}}
/Users/bogdan.didenko/lpnu/review/data/docling_uncertain_no_vlm_2026-07-08/chunks/full_2026_07_06_rec_003118.jsonl:106:{"candidate_id": "full_2026-07-06__rec_003118", "chunk_index": 106, "text": "MOP. The MOP (Zhang et al., 2023) dataset is a spatially resolved, molecularly defined cell atlas of an entire mouse brain. This dataset provides 338 major cell types over ten million cells across eleven major brain regions. The MOP dataset was collected by the Multiplexed Error-Robust Fluorescence In Situ Hybridization (MERFISH) technology, which is a spatial transcriptomics (ST) method that allows for gene expression profiling while preserving the spatial context of the cells within intact tissue sections. MOP is utilized for the CTA task in this paper.\nAirway. The Airway (Montoro et al., 2018) dataset is profiled by scRNA-seq protocol and comprises 7,494 cells from mice. The dataset revealed seven cell types including basal cells, club cells, ciliated cells, tuft cells, neuroendocrine, goblet cells, and Foxi1+ pulmonary ionocyte cells. Airway is utilized for evaluating the RCI task in this study.", "contextualized_text": "B EVALUATION PROTOCOLS\nB.2 EVALUATION DATASETS\nMOP. The MOP (Zhang et al., 2023) dataset is a spatially resolved, molecularly defined cell atlas of an entire mouse brain. This dataset provides 338 major cell types over ten million cells across eleven major brain regions. The MOP dataset was collected by the Multiplexed Error-Robust Fluorescence In Situ Hybridization (MERFISH) technology, which is a spatial transcriptomics (ST) method that allows for gene expression profiling while preserving the spatial context of the cells within intact tissue sections. MOP is utilized for the CTA task in this paper.\nAirway. The Airway (Montoro et al., 2018) dataset is profiled by scRNA-seq protocol and comprises 7,494 cells from mice. The dataset revealed seven cell types including basal cells, club cells, ciliated cells, tuft cells, neuroendocrine, goblet cells, and Foxi1+ pulmonary ionocyte cells. Airway is utilized for evaluating the RCI task in this study.", "headings": ["B EVALUATION PROTOCOLS", "B.2 EVALUATION DATASETS"], "pages": [17], "meta": {"schema_name": "docling_core.transforms.chunker.DocMeta", "version": "1.0.0", "doc_items": [{"self_ref": "#/texts/969", "parent": {"cref": "#/body"}, "children": [], "content_layer": "body", "meta": null, "label": "text", "prov": [{"page_no": 17, "bbox": {"l": 108.0, "t": 218.25819400000057, "r": 504.00338739999967, "b": 154.52377439292093, "coord_origin": "BOTTOMLEFT"}, "charspan": [0, 561]}]}, {"self_ref": "#/texts/970", "parent": {"cref": "#/body"}, "children": [], "content_layer": "body", "meta": null, "label": "text", "prov": [{"page_no": 17, "bbox": {"l": 108.0, "t": 138.1561940000006, "r": 504.0033873999996, "b": 96.33877439292087, "coord_origin": "BOTTOMLEFT"}, "charspan": [0, 350]}]}], "headings": ["B EVALUATION PROTOCOLS", "B.2 EVALUATION DATASETS"], "captions": null, "origin": {"mimetype": "application/pdf", "binary_hash": 7109630141359406345, "filename": "full_text_user_manual_zeromics.pdf", "uri": null}}}
/Users/bogdan.didenko/lpnu/review/data/docling_uncertain_no_vlm_2026-07-08/chunks/full_2026_07_06_rec_003118.jsonl:107:{"candidate_id": "full_2026-07-06__rec_003118", "chunk_index": 107, "text": "CTC. The CTC (Szczerba et al., 2019) dataset focuses on circulating tumor cells (CTCs) associated with white blood cells (WBCs), specifically neutrophils, in patients with breast cancer. This\n918\n919\n920\n921\n922\n923\n924\n925\n926\n927\n928\n929\n930\n931\n932\n933\n934\n935\n936\n937\n938\n939\n940\n941\n942\n943\n944\n945\n946\n947\n948\n949\n950\n951\n952\n953\n954\n955\n956\n957\n958\n959\n960\n961\n962\n963\n964\n965\n966\n967\n968\n969\n970\n971\ndataset is profiled by Smart-seq2 and contains 357 cells. There is no annotation file about cell subtypes attached to the dataset, but it provides a binary annotation of whether it is a tumor or not. CTC is used for evaluating the TCD task.", "contextualized_text": "B EVALUATION PROTOCOLS\nB.2 EVALUATION DATASETS\nCTC. The CTC (Szczerba et al., 2019) dataset focuses on circulating tumor cells (CTCs) associated with white blood cells (WBCs), specifically neutrophils, in patients with breast cancer. This\n918\n919\n920\n921\n922\n923\n924\n925\n926\n927\n928\n929\n930\n931\n932\n933\n934\n935\n936\n937\n938\n939\n940\n941\n942\n943\n944\n945\n946\n947\n948\n949\n950\n951\n952\n953\n954\n955\n956\n957\n958\n959\n960\n961\n962\n963\n964\n965\n966\n967\n968\n969\n970\n971\ndataset is profiled by Smart-seq2 and contains 357 cells. There is no annotation file about cell subtypes attached to the dataset, but it provides a binary annotation of whether it is a tumor or not. CTC is used for evaluating the TCD task.", "headings": ["B EVALUATION PROTOCOLS", "B.2 EVALUATION DATASETS"], "pages": [17, 18], "meta": {"schema_name": "docling_core.transforms.chunker.DocMeta", "version": "1.0.0", "doc_items": [{"self_ref": "#/texts/971", "parent": {"cref": "#/body"}, "children": [], "content_layer": "body", "meta": null, "label": "text", "p