body"}, "children": [], "content_layer": "body", "meta": null, "label": "text", "prov": [{"page_no": 18, "bbox": {"l": 73.131, "t": 88.8884121999995, "r": 86.42512679999999, "b": 81.40448829999957, "coord_origin": "BOTTOMLEFT"}, "charspan": [0, 3]}]}, {"self_ref": "#/texts/1027", "parent": {"cref": "#/body"}, "children": [], "content_layer": "body", "meta": null, "label": "text", "prov": [{"page_no": 18, "bbox": {"l": 73.131, "t": 76.93341219999957, "r": 86.42512679999999, "b": 69.44948829999953, "coord_origin": "BOTTOMLEFT"}, "charspan": [0, 3]}]}, {"self_ref": "#/texts/1028", "parent": {"cref": "#/body"}, "children": [], "content_layer": "body", "meta": null, "label": "text", "prov": [{"page_no": 18, "bbox": {"l": 108.0, "t": 706.6616526, "r": 504.0033873999993, "b": 676.1917743929205, "coord_origin": "BOTTOMLEFT"}, "charspan": [0, 240]}]}], "headings": ["B EVALUATION PROTOCOLS", "B.2 EVALUATION DATASETS"], "captions": null, "origin": {"mimetype": "application/pdf", "binary_hash": 7109630141359406345, "filename": "full_text_user_manual_zeromics.pdf", "uri": null}}}
/Users/bogdan.didenko/lpnu/review/data/docling_uncertain_no_vlm_2026-07-08/chunks/full_2026_07_06_rec_003118.jsonl:108:{"candidate_id": "full_2026-07-06__rec_003118", "chunk_index": 108, "text": "LungCancer. The LungCancer (Qian et al., 2020) dataset consists of scRNA-seq profiles of 93,576 cells derived from patients with lung cancer. The dataset identified ten distinct cell types including tumors. LungCancer is used for evaluating the TCD task in this study.\nHDHC. The HDHC (Chaffin et al., 2022) dataset consists of single-nucleus RNA sequencing (snRNA-seq) profiles of nearly 600,000 nuclei derived from the left ventricle samples of patients with hypertrophic cardiomyopathy and dilated cardiomyopathy (HDHC), and non-failing (NF) hearts. The dataset contains twenty-one distinct cell populations. HDHC is utilized for the CPI task in this study.\nLiver. The Liver (MacParland et al., 2018) dataset is profiled by scRNA-seq technology, comprising 8,444 cells isolated from healthy human liver tissues obtained from five neurologically deceased donors. The dataset identified twenty cell types in total. Liver is used for the CPI task in this study.", "contextualized_text": "B EVALUATION PROTOCOLS\nB.2 EVALUATION DATASETS\nLungCancer. The LungCancer (Qian et al., 2020) dataset consists of scRNA-seq profiles of 93,576 cells derived from patients with lung cancer. The dataset identified ten distinct cell types including tumors. LungCancer is used for evaluating the TCD task in this study.\nHDHC. The HDHC (Chaffin et al., 2022) dataset consists of single-nucleus RNA sequencing (snRNA-seq) profiles of nearly 600,000 nuclei derived from the left ventricle samples of patients with hypertrophic cardiomyopathy and dilated cardiomyopathy (HDHC), and non-failing (NF) hearts. The dataset contains twenty-one distinct cell populations. HDHC is utilized for the CPI task in this study.\nLiver. The Liver (MacParland et al., 2018) dataset is profiled by scRNA-seq technology, comprising 8,444 cells isolated from healthy human liver tissues obtained from five neurologically deceased donors. The dataset identified twenty cell types in total. Liver is used for the CPI task in this study.", "headings": ["B EVALUATION PROTOCOLS", "B.2 EVALUATION DATASETS"], "pages": [18], "meta": {"schema_name": "docling_core.transforms.chunker.DocMeta", "version": "1.0.0", "doc_items": [{"self_ref": "#/texts/1029", "parent": {"cref": "#/body"}, "children": [], "content_layer": "body", "meta": null, "label": "text", "prov": [{"page_no": 18, "bbox": {"l": 107.99999999999999, "t": 661.1711940000001, "r": 504.00338739999927, "b": 630.3127743929206, "coord_origin": "BOTTOMLEFT"}, "charspan": [0, 268]}]}, {"self_ref": "#/texts/1030", "parent": {"cref": "#/body"}, "children": [], "content_layer": "body", "meta": null, "label": "text", "prov": [{"page_no": 18, "bbox": {"l": 107.99999999999997, "t": 615.2921940000002, "r": 504.0033873999996, "b": 562.5157743929208, "coord_origin": "BOTTOMLEFT"}, "charspan": [0, 390]}]}, {"self_ref": "#/texts/1031", "parent": {"cref": "#/body"}, "children": [], "content_layer": "body", "meta": null, "label": "text", "prov": [{"page_no": 18, "bbox": {"l": 107.99999999999997, "t": 547.4951940000004, "r": 504.0033873999996, "b": 516.6367743929209, "coord_origin": "BOTTOMLEFT"}, "charspan": [0, 300]}]}], "headings": ["B EVALUATION PROTOCOLS", "B.2 EVALUATION DATASETS"], "captions": null, "origin": {"mimetype": "application/pdf", "binary_hash": 7109630141359406345, "filename": "full_text_user_manual_zeromics.pdf", "uri": null}}}
/Users/bogdan.didenko/lpnu/review/data/docling_uncertain_no_vlm_2026-07-08/chunks/full_2026_07_06_rec_003118.jsonl:109:{"candidate_id": "full_2026-07-06__rec_003118", "chunk_index": 109, "text": "All experiments are conducted on eight NVIDIA Tesla A100 GPUs, each with 80 GB of memory. The various parameter versions of ZerOmics, along with a series of its variant models are trained using the PyTorch framework (Paszke et al., 2019), integrated with DeepSpeed (Rasley et al., 2020) and FlashAttention v2 (Dao, 2024) for optimized memory and computational efficiency. Gradient checkpointing is employed by default, a widely adopted technique in the PEFT (Parameter-Efficient Fine-Tuning) codebase (Mangrulkar et al., 2022), to further reduce memory overhead during training. Please note that eight A100 GPUs are not strictly necessary; they are mainly used to accelerate the training process through parallelization.", "contextualized_text": "C IMPLEMENTATION DETAILS\nC.1 ENVIRONMENTS\nAll experiments are conducted on eight NVIDIA Tesla A100 GPUs, each with 80 GB of memory. The various parameter versions of ZerOmics, along with a series of its variant models are trained using the PyTorch framework (Paszke et al., 2019), integrated with DeepSpeed (Rasley et al., 2020) and FlashAttention v2 (Dao, 2024) for optimized memory and computational efficiency. Gradient checkpointing is employed by default, a widely adopted technique in the PEFT (Parameter-Efficient Fine-Tuning) codebase (Mangrulkar et al., 2022), to further reduce memory overhead during training. Please note that eight A100 GPUs are not strictly necessary; they are mainly used to accelerate the training process through parallelization.", "headings": ["C IMPLEMENTATION DETAILS", "C.1 ENVIRONMENTS"], "pages": [18], "meta": {"schema_name": "docling_core.transforms.chunker.DocMeta", "version": "1.0.0", "doc_items": [{"self_ref": "#/texts/1034", "parent": {"cref": "#/body"}, "children": [], "content_layer": "body", "meta": null, "label": "text", "prov": [{"page_no": 18, "bbox": {"l": 107.99999999999996, "t": 451.51365260000057, "r": 504.00338739999967, "b": 366.2487743929209, "coord_origin": "BOTTOMLEFT"}, "charspan": [0, 720]}]}], "headings": ["C IMPLEMENTATION DETAILS", "C.1 ENVIRONMENTS"], "captions": null, "origin": {"mimetype": "application/pdf", "binary_hash": 7109630141359406345, "filename": "full_text_user_manual_zeromics.pdf", "uri": null}}}
/Users/bogdan.didenko/lpnu/review/data/docling_uncertain_no_vlm_2026-07-08/chunks/full_2026_07_06_rec_003118.jsonl:110:{"candidate_id": "full_2026-07-06__rec_003118", "chunk_index": 110, "text": "In this section, we offer a concise overview of each benchmark method utilized in this study.\nSpatial-ID. Spatial-ID (Shen et al., 2022) is a supervised benchmark for the CTA task, which is tailored for ST data. It first employs transfer learning to train a deep neural network (DNN) pretrained on the reference scRNA-seq data. In the inference stage, it leverages a variational graph autoencoder (VGAE) to contain spot embeddings, followed by feeding them into the DNN-based classifier to generate pseudo-labels for each spot. The spatial embeddings are then combined with gene expression profiles to refine cell type predictions via a self-supervised learning approach.", "contextualized_text": "C IMPLEMENTATION DETAILS\nC.2 BENCHMARK METHODS\nIn this section, we offer a concise overview of each benchmark method utilized in this study.\nSpatial-ID. Spatial-ID (Shen et al., 2022) is a supervised benchmark for the CTA task, which is tailored for ST data. It first employs transfer learning to train a deep neural network (DNN) pretrained on the reference scRNA-seq data. In the inference stage, it leverages a variational graph autoencoder (VGAE) to contain spot embeddings, followed by feeding them into the DNN-based classifier to generate pseudo-labels for each spot. The spatial embeddings are then combined with gene expression profiles to refine cell type predictions via a self-supervised learning approach.", "headings": ["C IMPLEMENTATION DETAILS", "C.2 BENCHMARK METHODS"], "pages": [18], "meta": {"schema_name": "docling_core.transforms.chunker.DocMeta", "version": "1.0.0", "doc_items": [{"self_ref": "#/texts/1036", "parent": {"cref": "#/body"}, "children": [], "content_layer": "body", "meta": null, "label": "text", "prov": [{"page_no": 18, "bbox": {"l": 107.99999999999994, "t": 328.85165260000053, "r": 476.4866861999995, "b": 320.2997743929209, "coord_origin": "BOTTOMLEFT"}, "charspan": [0, 93]}]}, {"self_ref": "#/texts/1037", "parent": {"cref": "#/body"}, "children": [], "content_layer": "body", "meta": null, "label": "text", "prov": [{"page_no": 18, "bbox": {"l": 107.99999999999994, "t": 305.27919400000053, "r": 504.0033873999997, "b": 241.54477439292089, "coord_origin": "BOTTOMLEFT"}, "charspan": [0, 577]}]}], "headings": ["C IMPLEMENTATION DETAILS", "C.2 BENCHMARK METHODS"], "captions": null, "origin": {"mimetype": "application/pdf", "binary_hash": 7109630141359406345, "filename": "full_text_user_manual_zeromics.pdf", "uri": null}}}
/Users/bogdan.didenko/lpnu/review/data/docling_uncertain_no_vlm_2026-07-08/chunks/full_2026_07_06_rec_003118.jsonl:111:{"candidate_id": "full_2026-07-06__rec_003118", "chunk_index": 111, "text": "scJoint. scJoint (Lin et al., 2022) is designed for the CTA task for both scRNA-seq and scATACseq data. Initially, it leverages annotated scRNA-seq data to guide the training process, transferring labels to unlabeled scATAC-seq data via constructing a KNN graph based on cell-cell similarities between two omics. scJoint simultaneously trains on labeled and unlabeled data, enabling effective label transfer and integration across heterogeneous multi-omics datasets.\nSeurat. Seurat v5 (Hao et al., 2023) serves as one of the baseline methods for the unsupervised CTA task. It first builds a K-nearest neighbor (KNN) graph based on cell-cell similarities, followed by community detection to annotate cells into subtypes via the Louvain algorithm.", "contextualized_text": "C IMPLEMENTATION DETAILS\nC.2 BENCHMARK METHODS\nscJoint. scJoint (Lin et al., 2022) is designed for the CTA task for both scRNA-seq and scATACseq data. Initially, it leverages annotated scRNA-seq data to guide the training process, transferring labels to unlabeled scATAC-seq data via constructing a KNN graph based on cell-cell similarities between two omics. scJoint simultaneously trains on labeled and unlabeled data, enabling effective label transfer and integration across heterogeneous multi-omics datasets.\nSeurat. Seurat v5 (Hao et al., 2023) serves as one of the baseline methods for the unsupervised CTA task. It first builds a K-nearest neighbor (KNN) graph based on cell-cell similarities, followed by community detection to annotate cells into subtypes via the Louvain algorithm.", "headings": ["C IMPLEMENTATION DETAILS", "C.2 BENCHMARK METHODS"], "pages": [18], "meta": {"schema_name": "docling_core.transforms.chunker.DocMeta", "version": "1.0.0", "doc_items": [{"self_ref": "#/texts/1038", "parent": {"cref": "#/body"}, "children": [], "content_layer": "body", "meta": null, "label": "text", "prov": [{"page_no": 18, "bbox": {"l": 107.99999999999994, "t": 226.52419400000053, "r": 504.0033873999995, "b": 173.74777439292086, "coord_origin": "BOTTOMLEFT"}, "charspan": [0, 466]}]}, {"self_ref": "#/texts/1039", "parent": {"cref": "#/body"}, "children": [], "content_layer": "body", "meta": null, "label": "text", "prov": [{"page_no": 18, "bbox": {"l": 107.99999999999994, "t": 158.7271940000005, "r": 504.00338739999927, "b": 127.86877439292084, "coord_origin": "BOTTOMLEFT"}, "charspan": [0, 278]}]}], "headings": ["C IMPLEMENTATION DETAILS", "C.2 BENCHMARK METHODS"], "captions": null, "origin": {"mimetype": "application/pdf", "binary_hash": 7109630141359406345, "filename": "full_text_user_manual_zeromics.pdf", "uri": null}}}
/Users/bogdan.didenko/lpnu/review/data/docling_uncertain_no_vlm_2026-07-08/chunks/full_2026_07_06_rec_003118.jsonl:112:{"candidate_id": "full_2026-07-06__rec_003118", "chunk_index": 112, "text": "scBERT. scBERT (Yang et al., 2022), as the pioneer of large-scale pre-trained models for SC data, utilizes the performer (Choromanski et al., 2020) architecture with 6M parameters and pre-trained on over 1M unlabeled, preprocessed scRNA-seq samples. At the supervised fine-tuning stage, the pre-trained encoder is tuned with labeled task-specific scRNA-seq data to adapt distinct downstream tasks. We employ scBERT as a baseline method for the CTA task in this study.\n972\n973\n974\n975\n976\n977\n978\n979\n980\n981\n982\n983\n984\n985\n986\n987\n988\n989\n990\n991\n992\n993\n994\n995\n996\n997\n998\n999\n1000\n1001\n1002\n1003\n1004\n1005\n1006\n1007\n1008\n1009\n1010\n1011\n1012\n1013\n1014\n1015\n1016\n1017\n1018\n1019\n1020\n1021\n1022\n1023\n1024\n1025", "contextualized_text": "C IMPLEMENTATION DETAILS\nC.2 BENCHMARK METHODS\nscBERT. scBERT (Yang et al., 2022), as the pioneer of large-scale pre-trained models for SC data, utilizes the performer (Choromanski et al., 2020) architecture with 6M parameters and pre-trained on over 1M unlabeled, preprocessed scRNA-seq samples. At the supervised fine-tuning stage, the pre-trained encoder is tuned with labeled task-specific scRNA-seq data to adapt distinct downstream tasks. We employ scBERT as a baseline method for the CTA task in this study.\n972\n973\n974\n975\n976\n977\n978\n979\n980\n981\n982\n983\n984\n985\n986\n987\n988\n989\n990\n991\n992\n993\n994\n995\n996\n997\n998\n999\n1000\n1001\n1002\n1003\n1004\n1005\n1006\n1007\n1008\n1009\n1010\n1011\n1012\n1013\n1014\n1015\n1016\n1017\n1018\n1019\n1020\n1021\n1022\n1023\n1024\n1025", "headings": ["C IMPLEMENTATION DETAILS", "C.2 BENCHMARK METHODS"], "pages": [18, 19], "meta": {"schema_name": "docling_core.transforms.chunker.DocMeta", "version": "1.0.0", "doc_items": [{"self_ref":