8", "parent": {"cref": "#/body"}, "children": [], "content_layer": "body", "meta": null, "label": "text", "prov": [{"page_no": 19, "bbox": {"l": 73.131, "t": 172.57441219999953, "r": 90.85650239999998, "b": 165.0904882999996, "coord_origin": "BOTTOMLEFT"}, "charspan": [0, 4]}]}, {"self_ref": "#/texts/1089", "parent": {"cref": "#/body"}, "children": [], "content_layer": "body", "meta": null, "label": "text", "prov": [{"page_no": 19, "bbox": {"l": 73.131, "t": 160.6194121999995, "r": 90.85650239999998, "b": 153.13548829999957, "coord_origin": "BOTTOMLEFT"}, "charspan": [0, 4]}]}, {"self_ref": "#/texts/1090", "parent": {"cref": "#/body"}, "children": [], "content_layer": "body", "meta": null, "label": "text", "prov": [{"page_no": 19, "bbox": {"l": 73.131, "t": 148.66441219999956, "r": 90.85650239999998, "b": 141.18048829999952, "coord_origin": "BOTTOMLEFT"}, "charspan": [0, 4]}]}, {"self_ref": "#/texts/1091", "parent": {"cref": "#/body"}, "children": [], "content_layer": "body", "meta": null, "label": "text", "prov": [{"page_no": 19, "bbox": {"l": 73.131, "t": 136.70841219999954, "r": 90.85650239999998, "b": 129.2244882999995, "coord_origin": "BOTTOMLEFT"}, "charspan": [0, 4]}]}, {"self_ref": "#/texts/1092", "parent": {"cref": "#/body"}, "children": [], "content_layer": "body", "meta": null, "label": "text", "prov": [{"page_no": 19, "bbox": {"l": 73.131, "t": 124.7534121999995, "r": 90.85650239999998, "b": 117.26948829999947, "coord_origin": "BOTTOMLEFT"}, "charspan": [0, 4]}]}, {"self_ref": "#/texts/1093", "parent": {"cref": "#/body"}, "children": [], "content_layer": "body", "meta": null, "label": "text", "prov": [{"page_no": 19, "bbox": {"l": 73.131, "t": 112.79841219999958, "r": 90.85650239999998, "b": 105.31448829999954, "coord_origin": "BOTTOMLEFT"}, "charspan": [0, 4]}]}, {"self_ref": "#/texts/1094", "parent": {"cref": "#/body"}, "children": [], "content_layer": "body", "meta": null, "label": "text", "prov": [{"page_no": 19, "bbox": {"l": 73.131, "t": 100.84341219999953, "r": 90.85650239999998, "b": 93.3594882999995, "coord_origin": "BOTTOMLEFT"}, "charspan": [0, 4]}]}, {"self_ref": "#/texts/1095", "parent": {"cref": "#/body"}, "children": [], "content_layer": "body", "meta": null, "label": "text", "prov": [{"page_no": 19, "bbox": {"l": 73.131, "t": 88.8884121999995, "r": 90.85650239999998, "b": 81.40448829999957, "coord_origin": "BOTTOMLEFT"}, "charspan": [0, 4]}]}, {"self_ref": "#/texts/1096", "parent": {"cref": "#/body"}, "children": [], "content_layer": "body", "meta": null, "label": "text", "prov": [{"page_no": 19, "bbox": {"l": 73.131, "t": 76.93341219999957, "r": 90.85650239999998, "b": 69.44948829999953, "coord_origin": "BOTTOMLEFT"}, "charspan": [0, 4]}]}], "headings": ["C IMPLEMENTATION DETAILS", "C.2 BENCHMARK METHODS"], "captions": null, "origin": {"mimetype": "application/pdf", "binary_hash": 7109630141359406345, "filename": "full_text_user_manual_zeromics.pdf", "uri": null}}}
/Users/bogdan.didenko/lpnu/review/data/docling_uncertain_no_vlm_2026-07-08/chunks/full_2026_07_06_rec_003118.jsonl:113:{"candidate_id": "full_2026-07-06__rec_003118", "chunk_index": 113, "text": "Geneformer. Geneformer (Theodoris et al., 2023) is pre-trained on nearly 30M scRNA-seq data samples, and adopts a CNN-based feature generator to learn cell representations, followed by a transformer-based entropy model. The model employs a latent array to manage sequence length to solve the gene compression problem. Finally, Geneformer applies transfer learning across various biological tasks. Geneformer is included as a benchmark for the CTA and CPI tasks in this study.\nscGPT. scGPT (Cui et al., 2024) serves as a benchmark method for CTA and RCI tasks in this paper. The model is generatively pre-trained on over 33M scRNA-seq samples, followed by supervised fine-tuning for specific downstream tasks, including CTA and batch integration.", "contextualized_text": "C IMPLEMENTATION DETAILS\nC.2 BENCHMARK METHODS\nGeneformer. Geneformer (Theodoris et al., 2023) is pre-trained on nearly 30M scRNA-seq data samples, and adopts a CNN-based feature generator to learn cell representations, followed by a transformer-based entropy model. The model employs a latent array to manage sequence length to solve the gene compression problem. Finally, Geneformer applies transfer learning across various biological tasks. Geneformer is included as a benchmark for the CTA and CPI tasks in this study.\nscGPT. scGPT (Cui et al., 2024) serves as a benchmark method for CTA and RCI tasks in this paper. The model is generatively pre-trained on over 33M scRNA-seq samples, followed by supervised fine-tuning for specific downstream tasks, including CTA and batch integration.", "headings": ["C IMPLEMENTATION DETAILS", "C.2 BENCHMARK METHODS"], "pages": [19], "meta": {"schema_name": "docling_core.transforms.chunker.DocMeta", "version": "1.0.0", "doc_items": [{"self_ref": "#/texts/1097", "parent": {"cref": "#/body"}, "children": [], "content_layer": "body", "meta": null, "label": "text", "prov": [{"page_no": 19, "bbox": {"l": 107.99999999999999, "t": 707.050194, "r": 504.0033873999997, "b": 654.2737743929206, "coord_origin": "BOTTOMLEFT"}, "charspan": [0, 475]}]}, {"self_ref": "#/texts/1098", "parent": {"cref": "#/body"}, "children": [], "content_layer": "body", "meta": null, "label": "text", "prov": [{"page_no": 19, "bbox": {"l": 107.99999999999999, "t": 638.6271940000003, "r": 504.00338739999944, "b": 607.7697743929207, "coord_origin": "BOTTOMLEFT"}, "charspan": [0, 269]}]}], "headings": ["C IMPLEMENTATION DETAILS", "C.2 BENCHMARK METHODS"], "captions": null, "origin": {"mimetype": "application/pdf", "binary_hash": 7109630141359406345, "filename": "full_text_user_manual_zeromics.pdf", "uri": null}}}
/Users/bogdan.didenko/lpnu/review/data/docling_uncertain_no_vlm_2026-07-08/chunks/full_2026_07_06_rec_003118.jsonl:114:{"candidate_id": "full_2026-07-06__rec_003118", "chunk_index": 114, "text": "LangCell. Langcell (Zhao et al., 2024) is a recent work that integrates textual information with gene expression profiles during the pre-training stage. It builds a cell-text dataset utilized for pretraining vis four tasks, including mask gene modeling, cell-cell contrastive learning, cell-text contrastive learning, and cell-text matching to recognize the intricate relationships between SC and text modalities. We employ it as a benchmark method for the CTA task.\nscVI. scVI (Lopez et al., 2018), tailored for scRNA-seq data analysis, implements a completely probabilistic framework based on a hierarchical Bayesian model. The gene expression profiles are firstly encoded into low-dimensional embeddings and then decoded for computing posterior estimates of the distributional parameters for each gene in each cell. scVI is utilized as a benchmark for the RCI task.", "contextualized_text": "C IMPLEMENTATION DETAILS\nC.2 BENCHMARK METHODS\nLangCell. Langcell (Zhao et al., 2024) is a recent work that integrates textual information with gene expression profiles during the pre-training stage. It builds a cell-text dataset utilized for pretraining vis four tasks, including mask gene modeling, cell-cell contrastive learning, cell-text contrastive learning, and cell-text matching to recognize the intricate relationships between SC and text modalities. We employ it as a benchmark method for the CTA task.\nscVI. scVI (Lopez et al., 2018), tailored for scRNA-seq data analysis, implements a completely probabilistic framework based on a hierarchical Bayesian model. The gene expression profiles are firstly encoded into low-dimensional embeddings and then decoded for computing posterior estimates of the distributional parameters for each gene in each cell. scVI is utilized as a benchmark for the RCI task.", "headings": ["C IMPLEMENTATION DETAILS", "C.2 BENCHMARK METHODS"], "pages": [19], "meta": {"schema_name": "docling_core.transforms.chunker.DocMeta", "version": "1.0.0", "doc_items": [{"self_ref": "#/texts/1099", "parent": {"cref": "#/body"}, "children": [], "content_layer": "body", "meta": null, "label": "text", "prov": [{"page_no": 19, "bbox": {"l": 108.0, "t": 592.1231940000004, "r": 504.00338739999955, "b": 539.3477743929209, "coord_origin": "BOTTOMLEFT"}, "charspan": [0, 466]}]}, {"self_ref": "#/texts/1100", "parent": {"cref": "#/body"}, "children": [], "content_layer": "body", "meta": null, "label": "text", "prov": [{"page_no": 19, "bbox": {"l": 108.0, "t": 523.7011940000006, "r": 504.0033873999997, "b": 470.9247743929209, "coord_origin": "BOTTOMLEFT"}, "charspan": [0, 401]}]}], "headings": ["C IMPLEMENTATION DETAILS", "C.2 BENCHMARK METHODS"], "captions": null, "origin": {"mimetype": "application/pdf", "binary_hash": 7109630141359406345, "filename": "full_text_user_manual_zeromics.pdf", "uri": null}}}
/Users/bogdan.didenko/lpnu/review/data/docling_uncertain_no_vlm_2026-07-08/chunks/full_2026_07_06_rec_003118.jsonl:115:{"candidate_id": "full_2026-07-06__rec_003118", "chunk_index": 115, "text": "MARS. MARS(Brbi´ c et al., 2020) is a baseline method for the RCI task. MARS firstly predefines a set of cluster landmarks that are equal to the number of known cell types for the unannotated dataset. Subsequently, unlabeled cells are assigned to the cluster of the closest target landmark in the embedding space. The assigned cell clusters are matched to annotated cell-type landmarks in the annotated dataset, identifying those with uncertain matching as rare cell types.\nscBalance. scBalance (Cheng et al., 2023) is a framework specifically designed for the RCI task. It combines weight sampling and sparse neural networks to emphasize minor cell types without disrupting the annotation efficiency of the major cell populations. scBalance outperforms in handling imbalanced datasets, thus we use it as a benchmark for the RCI task.", "contextualized_text": "C IMPLEMENTATION DETAILS\nC.2 BENCHMARK METHODS\nMARS. MARS(Brbi´ c et al., 2020) is a baseline method for the RCI task. MARS firstly predefines a set of cluster landmarks that are equal to the number of known cell types for the unannotated dataset. Subsequently, unlabeled cells are assigned to the cluster of the closest target landmark in the embedding space. The assigned cell clusters are matched to annotated cell-type landmarks in the annotated dataset, identifying those with uncertain matching as rare cell types.\nscBalance. scBalance (Cheng et al., 2023) is a framework specifically designed for the RCI task. It combines weight sampling and sparse neural networks to emphasize minor cell types without disrupting the annotation efficiency of the major cell populations. scBalance outperforms in handling imbalanced datasets, thus we use it as a benchmark for the RCI task.", "headings": ["C IMPLEMENTATION DETAILS", "C.2 BENCHMARK METHODS"], "pages": [19], "meta": {"schema_name": "docling_core.transforms.chunker.DocMeta", "version": "1.0.0", "doc_items": [{"self_ref": "#/texts/1101", "parent": {"cref": "#/body"}, "children": [], "content_layer": "body", "meta": null, "label": "text", "prov": [{"page_no": 19, "bbox": {"l": 108.0, "t": 455.27919400000053, "r": 504.00338739999955, "b": 402.50277439292086, "coord_origin": "BOTTOMLEFT"}, "charspan": [0, 473]}]}, {"self_ref": "#/texts/1102", "parent": {"cref": "#/body"}, "children": [], "content_layer": "body", "meta": null, "label": "text", "prov": [{"page_no": 19, "bbox": {"l": 108.00000000000003, "t": 386.8571940000005, "r": 504.0033873999994, "b": 345.03977439292083, "coord_origin": "BOTTOMLEFT"}, "charspan": [0, 360]}]}], "headings": ["C IMPLEMENTATION DETAILS", "C.2 BENCHMARK METHODS"], "captions": null, "origin": {"mimetype": "application/pdf", "binary_hash": 7109630141359406345, "filename": "full_text_user_manual_zeromics.pdf", "uri": null}}}
/Users/bogdan.didenko/lpnu/review/data/docling_uncertain_no_vlm_2026-07-08/chunks/full_2026_07_06_rec_003118.jsonl:116:{"candidate_id": "full_2026-07-06__rec_003118", "chunk_index": 116, "text": "CopyKAT. CopyKAT (Gao et al., 2021) serves as a baseline method for the TCD task. It performs hierarchical clustering to categorize cells into clusters according to their estimated gene copy number profiles. Clusters that are significantly enriched in predefined highly confident normal spots in the enrichment analysis (P-value ≤ 0.05) are designated as normal cells and others as tumors.\nCaSee. CaSee (Sh et al., 2022), tailored for distinguishing tumors from normal cells, pre-training the model on a vast of bulk RNA-seq data, followed by employing transfer learning to detect tumors in scRNA-seq data processed by a capsule network. CaSee is a baseline method for the TCD task in this study.", "contextualized_text": "C IMPLEMENTATION DETAILS\nC.2 BENCHMARK METHODS\nCopyKAT. CopyKAT (Gao et al., 2021) serves as a baseline method for the TCD task. It performs hierarchical clustering to categorize cells into clusters according to their estimated gene copy number profiles. Clusters that are significantly enriched in predefined highly confident normal spots in the enrichment analysis (P-value ≤ 0.05) are designated as normal cells and others as tumors.\nCaSee. CaSee (Sh et al., 2022), tailored for distinguishing tumors from normal cells, pre-training the model on a vast of bulk RNA-seq data, followed by employing transfer learning to detect tumors in scRNA-seq data processed by a capsule network. CaSee is a baseline method for the TCD task in this study.", "headings": ["C IMPLEMENTATION DETAILS", "C.2 BENCHMARK METHODS"], "pages": [19], "meta": {"schema_name": "docling_core.transforms.chunker.DocMeta", "version": "1.0.0", "doc_items": [{"self_ref": "#/texts/1103", "parent": {"cref": "#/body"}, "children": [], "content_layer": "body", "meta": null, "label": "text", "prov": [{"page_no": 19, "bbox": {"l": 108.00000000000003, "t": 329.3941940000005, "r": 504.0034613999998, "b": 287.5767743929208, "coord_origin": "BOTTOMLEFT"}, "charspan": [0, 389]}]}, {"self_ref": "#/texts/1104", "parent": {"cref": "#/body"}, "children": [], "content_layer": "body", "meta": null, "label": "text", "prov": [{"page_no": 19, "bbox": {"l": 108.00000000000003, "t": 271.9301940000005, "r": 504.0033873999996, "b": 230.11377439292085, "coord_origin": "BOTTOMLEFT"}, "charspan": [0, 306]}]}], "headings": ["C IMPLEMENTATION DETAILS", "C.2 BENCHMARK METHODS"], "captions": null, "origin": {"mimetype": "application/pdf", "binary_hash": 7109630141359406345, "filename": "full_text_user_manual_zeromics.pdf", "uri": null}}}
/Users/bogdan.didenko/lpnu/review/data/docling_uncertain_no_vlm_2026-07-08/chunks/full_2026_07_06_rec_003118.jsonl:117:{"candidate_id": "full_2026-07-06__rec_003118", "chunk_index": 117, "text": "ikarus. ikarus (Dohmen et al., 2022) is designed for detecting tumors from normal cells at the SC level. It first identifies a comprehensive tumor cell signature in the form of a gene set by consolidating abundant annotated datasets. It then employs a logistic regression classifier for stringent discrimination of tumor and normal cells, supplemented by a network-based propagation of cell labels using a custom-built cell-cell network. We leverage ikarus as a baseline for the TCD task in this study.", "contextualized_text": "C IMPLEMENTATION DETAILS\nC.2 BENCHMARK METHODS\nikarus. ikarus (Dohmen et al., 2022) is designed for detecting tumors from normal cells at the SC level. It first identifies a comprehensive tumor cell signature in the form of a gene set by consolidating abundant annotated datasets. It then employs a logistic regression classifier for stringent discrimination of tumor and normal cells, supplemented by a network-based propagation of cell labels using a custom-built cell-cell network. We leverage ikarus as a baseline for the TCD task in this study.", "headings": ["C IMPLEMENTATION DETAILS", "C.2 BENCHMARK METHODS"], "pages": [19], "meta": {"schema_name": "docling_core.transforms.chunker.DocMeta", "version": "1.0.0", "doc_items": [{"self_ref": "#/texts/1105", "parent": {"cref": "#/body"}, "children": [], "content_layer": "body", "meta": null, "label": "text", "prov": [{"page_no": 19, "bbox": {"l": 108.00000000000001, "t": 214.46719400000052, "r": 504.0033873999997, "b": 150.73277439292087, "coord_origin": "BOTTOMLEFT"}, "charspan": [0, 502]}]}], "headings": ["C IMPLEMENTATION DETAILS", "C.2 BENCHMARK METHODS"], "captions": null, "origin": {"mimetype": "application/pdf", "binary_hash": 7109630141359406345, "filename": "full_text_user_manual_zeromics.pdf", "uri": null}}}
/Users/bogdan.didenko/lpnu/review/data/docling_uncertain_no_vlm_2026-07-08/chunks/full_2026_07_06_rec_003118.jsonl:118:{"candidate_id": "full_2026-07-06__rec_003118", "chunk_index": 118, "text": "In the single-cell model, the number of bins is set to 10. Therefore, the SC expression is processed into 12 different types of tokens: 10 bin tokens, a zero value token, and a special mask token. Then, the dimensions of SC expression embeddings are set the same as text embeddings for addition by the broadcasting mechanism. The main network of the SC Model is a stack of multiple layers of Transformer Encoders. We set it to 12 layers of Transformer Encoders, with 12 attention heads in\n1026 1027 1028 1029 1030 1031 1032 1033 1034 1035 1036 1037 1038 1039 1040 1041 1042 1043 1044 1045 1046 1047 1048 1049 1050 1051 1052 1053 1054 1055 1056 1057 1058 1059 1060 1061 1062 1063 1064 1065 1066 1067 1068 1069 1070 1071 1072 1073 1074 1075 1076 1077 1078 1079", "contextualized_text": "C IMPLEMENTATION DETAILS\nC.3 MODEL ARCHITECTURE AND TRAINING DETAILS\nIn the single-cell model, the number of bins is set to 10. Therefore, the SC expression is processed into 12 different types of tokens: 10 bin tokens, a zero value token, and a special mask token. Then, the dimensions of SC expression embeddings are set the same as text embeddings for addition by the broadcasting mechanism. The main network of the SC Model is a stack of multiple layers of Transformer Encoders. We set it to 12 layers of Transformer Encoders, with 12 attention heads in\n1026 1027 1028 1029 1030 1031 1032 1033 1034 1035 1036 1037 1038 1039 1040 1041 1042 1043 1044 1045 1046 1047 1048 1049 1050 1051 1052 1053 1054 1055 1056 1057 1058 1059 1060 1061 1062 1063 1064 1065 1066 1067 1068 1069 1070 1071 1072 1073 1074 1075 1076 1077 1078 1079", "headings": ["C IMPLEMENTATION DETAILS", "C.3 MODEL ARCHITECTURE AND TRAINING DETAILS"], "pages": [19, 20], "meta": {"schema_name": "docling_core.transforms.chunker.DocMeta", "version": "1.0.0", "doc_items": [{"self_ref": "#/texts/1107", "parent": {"cref": "#/body"}, "children": [], "content_layer": "body", "meta": null, "label": "text", "prov": [{"page_no": 19, "bbox": {"l": 108.0, "t": 112.45965260000048, "r": 504.00338739999967, "b": 60.071774392920815, "coord_origin": "BOTTOMLEFT"}, "charspan": [0, 488]}]}, {"self_ref": "#/texts/1110", "parent": {"cref": "#/body"}, "children": [], "content_layer": "body", "meta": null, "label": "text", "prov": [{"page_no": 20, "bbox": {"l": 73.131, "t": 710.5574122, "r": 90.85650239999998, "b": 69.44948829999953, "coord_origin": "BOTTOMLEFT"}, "charspan": [0, 269]}]}], "headings": ["C IMPLEMENTATION DETAILS", "C.3 MODEL ARCHITECTURE AND TRAINING DETAILS"], "captions": null, "origin": {"mimetype": "application/pdf", "binary_hash": 7109630141359406345, "filename": "full_text_user_manual_zeromics.pdf", "uri": null}}}
/Users/bogdan.didenko/lpnu/review/data/docling_uncertain_no_vlm_2026-07-08/chunks/full_2026_07_06_rec_003118.jsonl:119:{"candidate_id": "full_2026-07-06__rec_003118", "chunk_index": 119, "text": "each layer. In mask learning, we set p m = 0 . 15 for masking bin tokens and p s = 0 . 1 for randomly replacing tokens with others.\nFor the LLM, ZerOmics uses LLaMA2-13B by default. Each SC embedding is mapped to 500 LLM tokens through a linear layer (tokenizer) and inserted into the instruction. We select three representative and important SC analysis tasks: cell type annotation, rare cell identification, and tumor cell discovery. For each task, we also independently design three instruction templates. During fine-tuning, each cell is randomly assigned a task and a corresponding instruction. In the mixture of LoRAs, we design universal and three task-specific LoRAs, each with the same structure, using r = 8 and α = r .", "contextualized_text": "C IMPLEMENTATION DETAILS\nC.3 MODEL ARCHITECTURE AND TRAINING DETAILS\neach layer. In mask learning, we set p m = 0 . 15 for masking bin tokens and p s = 0 . 1 for randomly replacing tokens with others.\nFor the LLM, ZerOmics uses LLaMA2-13B by default. Each SC embedding is mapped to 500 LLM tokens through a linear layer (tokenizer) and inserted into the instruction. We select three representative and important SC analysis tasks: cell type annotation, rare cell identification, and tumor cell discovery. For each task, we also independently design three instruction templates. During fine-tuning, each cell is randomly assigned a task and a corresponding instruction. In the mixture of LoRAs, we design universal and three task-specific LoRAs, each with the same structure, using r = 8 and α = r .", "headings": ["C IMPLEMENTATION DETAILS", "C.3 MODEL ARCHITECTURE AND TRAINING DETAILS"], "pages": [20], "meta": {"schema_name": "docling_core.transforms.chunker.DocMeta", "version": "1.0.0", "doc_items": [{"self_ref": "#/texts/1111", "parent": {"cref": "#/body"}, "children": [], "content_layer": "body", "meta": null, "label": "text", "prov": [{"page_no": 20, "bbox": {"l": 107.99999999999994, "t": 706.9804558000001, "r": 503.9991263999998, "b": 687.1507743929204, "coord_origin": "BOTTOMLEFT"}, "charspan": [0, 131]}]}, {"self_ref": "#/texts/1112", "parent": {"cref": "#/body"}, "children": [], "content_layer": "body", "meta": null, "label": "text", "prov": [{"page_no": 20, "bbox": {"l": 107.99999999999994, "t": 678.7656526000001, "r": 504.00338739999955, "b": 604.4607743929207, "coord_origin": "BOTTOMLEFT"}, "charspan": [0, 597]}]}], "headings": ["C IMPLEMENTATION DETAILS", "C.3 MODEL ARCHITECTURE AND TRAINING DETAILS"], "captions": null, "origin": {"mimetype": "application/pdf", "binary_hash": 7109630141359406345, "filename": "full_text_user_manual_zeromics.pdf", "uri": null}}}
/Users/bogdan.didenko/lpnu/review/data/docling_uncertain_no_vlm_2026-07-08/chunks/full_2026_07_06_rec_003118.jsonl:120:{"candidate_id": "full_2026-07-06__rec_003118", "chunk_index": 120, "text": "For both pre-training and instruction tuning stages, we set the total batch size to 64, which means the pre-GPU batch size is 8. We use AdamW as the optimizer and the learning rate warmup and cosine decay strategies are also used in both stages. The learning rates of the two stages are different, the former is set to 1e-4, and the latter is set to 5e-4.", "contextualized_text": "C IMPLEMENTATION DETAILS\nC.3 MODEL ARCHITECTURE AND TRAINING DETAILS\nFor both pre-training and instruction tuning stages, we set the total batch size to 64, which means the pre-GPU batch size is 8. We use AdamW as the optimizer and the learning rate warmup and cosine decay strategies are also used in both stages. The learning rates of the two stages are different, the former is set to 1e-4, and the latter is set to 5e-4.", "headings": ["C IMPLEMENTATION DETAILS", "C.3 MODEL ARCHITECTURE AND TRAINING DETAILS"], "pages": [20], "meta": {"schema_name": "docling_core.transforms.chunker.DocMeta", "version": "1.0.0", "doc_items": [{"self_ref": "#/texts/1113", "parent": {"cref": "#/body"}, "children": [], "content_layer": "body", "meta": null, "label": "text", "prov": [{"page_no": 20, "bbox": {"l": 107.99999999999991, "t": 596.0756526000004, "r": 504.00338739999967, "b": 554.6477743929208, "coord_origin": "BOTTOMLEFT"}, "charspan": [0, 355]}]}], "headings": ["C IMPLEMENTATION DETAILS", "C.3 MODEL ARCHITECTURE AND TRAINING DETAILS"], "captions": null, "origin": {"mimetype": "application/pdf", "binary_hash": 7109630141359406345, "filename": "full_text_user_manual_zeromics.pdf", "uri": null}}}
/Users/bogdan.didenko/lpnu/review/data/docling_uncertain_no_vlm_2026-07-08/chunks/full_2026_07_06_rec_003118.jsonl:121:{"candidate_id": "full_2026-07-06__rec_003118", "chunk_index": 121, "text": "All of the code for this paper, including ZerOmics, its variant models, and most of the pre-training and fine-tuning weights, can be released once the paper is accepted.", "contextualized_text": "C IMPLEMENTATION DETAILS\nC.4 SOURCE CODE\nAll of the code for this paper, including ZerOmics, its variant models, and most of the pre-training and fine-tuning weights, can be released once the paper is accepted.", "headings": ["C IMPLEMENTATION DETAILS", "C.4 SOURCE CODE"], "pages": [20], "meta": {"schema_name": "docling_core.transforms.chunker.DocMeta", "version": "1.0.0", "doc_items": [{"self_ref": "#/texts/1115", "parent": {"cref": "#/body"}, "children": [], "content_layer": "body", "meta": null, "label": "text", "prov": [{"page_no": 20, "bbox": {"l": 107.99999999999991, "t": 517.6696526000005, "r": 504.0033873999992, "b": 498.15877439292086, "coord_origin": "BOTTOMLEFT"}, "charspan": [0, 169]}]}], "headings": ["C IMPLEMENTATION DETAILS", "C.4 SOURCE CODE"], "captions": null, "origin": {"mimetype": "application/pdf", "binary_hash": 7109630141359406345, "filename": "full_text_user_manual_zeromics.pdf", "uri": null}}}
/Users/bogdan.didenko/lpnu/review/data/docling_uncertain_no_vlm_2026-07-08/chunks/full_2026_07_06_rec_003118.jsonl:122:{"candidate_id": "full_2026-07-06__rec_003118", "chunk_index": 122, "text": "Some gene text summaries are presented in Table 6.", "contextualized_text": "D EXAMPLE ITEMS IN GENE CORPUS\nSome gene text summaries are presented in Table 6.", "headings": ["D EXAMPLE ITEMS IN GENE CORPUS"], "pages": [20], "meta": {"schema_name": "docling_core.transforms.chunker.DocMeta", "version": "1.0.0", "doc_items": [{"self_ref": "#/texts/1117", "parent": {"cref": "#/body"}, "children": [], "content_layer": "body", "meta": null, "label": "text", "prov": [{"page_no": 20, "bbox": {"l": 107.99999999999991, "t": 454.1586526000005, "r": 314.8435011999999, "b": 445.60677439292084, "coord_origin": "BOTTOMLEFT"}, "charspan": [0, 50]}]}], "headings": ["D EXAMPLE ITEMS IN GENE CORPUS"], "captions": null, "origin": {"mimetype": "application/pdf", "binary_hash": 7109630141359406345, "filename": "full_text_user_manual_zeromics.pdf", "uri": null}}}
/Users/bogdan.didenko/lpnu/review/data/docling_uncertain_no_vlm_2026-07-08/chunks/full_2026_07_06_rec_003118.jsonl:123:{"candidate_id": "full_2026-07-06__rec_003118", "chunk_index": 123, "text": "In the instruction tuning stage, ZerOmics employs three instruction tuning tasks. In the evaluation (also inference) stage, ZerOmics employs these three tasks with one additional unseen task. ZerOmics addresses these three shared tasks with similar instruction templates. In summary, the used instruction templates are presented in Table 7.\n1080\n1081\n1082\n1083\n1084\n1085\n1086\n1087\n1088\n1089\n1090\n1091\n1092\n1093\n1094\n1095\n1096\n1097\n1098\n1099\n1100\n1101\n1102\n1103\n1104\n1105\n1106\n1107\n1108\n1109\n1110\n1111\n1112\n1113\n1114\n1115\n1116\n1117\n1118\n1119\n1120\n1121\n1122\n1123\n1124\n1125\n1126\n1127\n1128\n1129\n1130\n1131\n1132\n1133\n", "contextualized_text": "E INSTRUCTION EXAMPLES\nIn the instruction tuning stage, ZerOmics employs three instruction tuning tasks. In the evaluation (also inference) stage, ZerOmics employs these three tasks with one additional unseen task. ZerOmics addresses these three shared tasks with similar instruction templates. In summary, the used instruction templates are presented in Table 7.\n1080\n1081\n1082\n1083\n1084\n1085\n1086\n1087\n1088\n1089\n1090\n1091\n1092\n1093\n1094\n1095\n1096\n1097\n1098\n1099\n1100\n1101\n1102\n1103\n1104\n1105\n1106\n1107\n1108\n1109\n1110\n1111\n1112\n1113\n1114\n1115\n1116\n1117\n1118\n1119\n1120\n1121\n1122\n1123\n1124\n1125\n1126\n1127\n1128\n1129\n1130\n1131\n1132\n1133\n", "headings": ["E INSTRUCTION EXAMPLES"], "pages": [20, 21], "meta": {"schema_name": "docling_core.transforms.chunker.DocMeta", "version": "1.0.0", "doc_items": [{"self_ref": "#/texts/1119", "parent": {"cref": "#/body"}, "children": [], "content_layer": "body", "meta": null, "label": "text", "prov": [{"page_no": 20, "bbox": {"l": 107.99999999999993, "t": 401.6056526000005, "r": 504.0033873999993, "b": 360.17677439292